        answer: "It was discovered in turkeys during the 1950s. When turkeys were switched to a new 'complete' feed-pellet ration, fully half of them died within the first thirteen weeks, and autopsies showed almost all had died of a ruptured aortic aneurysm. A pathologist reasoned it had to be copper deficiency, because copper is required to manufacture the heavy elastic fibers in artery walls \u2014 and when the copper in the feed was doubled the next year, not a single turkey died of a ruptured aneurysm.",
        verbatim: "In the first thirteen weeks on the original\ncomplete pellet ration, fully half of the turkeys on this ration died. Farmers\nwere picking them up by the bushel baskets full and taking them to the state\ndiagnostic lab to see what they died from.\nAt autopsy, it was obvious that almost all of the dead turkeys had died from\na ruptured aortic aneurysm. One clever pathologist said, \u201CThis has got to be\ndue to a copper deficiency, because copper is required to manufacture the\nheavy elastic fibers found in arteries.\u201D The next year the amount of copper\nwas doubled in the feed pellets and not a single turkey died from a ruptured\naneurysm.",
