        answer_short: "Wallach never wrote about microplastics, but they're a xenobiotic \u2014 a foreign chemical the body must clear \u2014 and he endorses selenium's role in the 'detoxification of metals and certain xenobiotics.'",
        answer: "Microplastics are a modern problem Wallach's books never take up. But they fall squarely into a category he does address: xenobiotics \u2014 the foreign compounds the body has to recognize and clear. In a selenium review he quotes approvingly across several books, the mineral is described as 'involved in detoxification of metals and certain xenobiotics,' one of the body's built-in tools for handling foreign chemicals. From there his general defense against toxin load is consistent: keep selenium up (he runs 250 to 500 mcg a day in his prevention programs), lean on plant-derived colloidal minerals to 'reduce your risk of damage from heavy metals and pollution,' filter your home's water and air, and run a periodic detox. None of this is Wallach speaking about plastics specifically \u2014 it is his framework for foreign-chemical exposure, which microplastics now fall under.",
        verbatim: "selenium acts as an acceptor of\nbiogenic methyl groups, and is involved in detoxification of metals and\ncertain xenobiotics. Selenium also has immunopotentiating properties.",
        page: null,
