        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "What causes night blindness?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says night blindness is caused by a vitamin A deficiency, from a poor diet, fat malabsorption, or a zinc shortage that blocks the liver from converting carotene to vitamin A.",
        answer: "Wallach teaches that night blindness (xerophthalmia) is caused by a vitamin A deficiency, which can arise several ways. It can come from an outright dietary shortage, from fat-malabsorption syndromes (vitamin A is fat-soluble), or from a zinc deficiency that leaves the liver unable to convert carotene into vitamin A. Conditions such as cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, and food allergies that damage the intestine and block absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, as well as a lack of essential fatty acids, can also cause it. He treats night blindness very specifically with 25,000 to 300,000 IU of vitamin A per day as beta-carotene plus zinc at 15 to 50 mg three times a day.",
        verbatim: "NIGHT BLINDNESS (xerophthalmia) is caused by a vitamin A deficiency\nwhich can be overt dietary deficiency, fat malabsorption syndrome (vitamin\nA is fat soluble), zinc deficiency which results in poor conversion of\ncarotene to vitamin A by the liver, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease and various\nfood allergies resulting in intestinal changes that prevent absorption of fat\nsoluble vitamins (i.e. vitamin A, vitamin E, vitamin D and vitamin K), as\nwell as a deficiency of essential fatty acids.",
