        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is calcium?",
        answer_short: "Calcium is the fifth most abundant mineral in the earth's crust and the most abundant mineral in the human body \u2014 the structural mineral of bones and teeth that also powers muscle contraction, blood clotting, nerve signaling and the heartbeat.",
        answer: "Wallach describes calcium as essential to all living organisms and the fifth most abundant mineral in the earth's crust and biosphere. It is also the most abundant mineral in the human body: about 99 percent of it forms the hydroxyapatite crystal that gives bones and teeth their structure, while the remaining one percent works as a cofactor and activator \u2014 regulating the electrochemical functions of cells, releasing energy from ATP (adenosine triphosphate) for muscle contraction, driving blood clotting, triggering neurotransmitter release, and helping regulate the heartbeat. He argues that humans are biochemically designed for high-calcium diets, that modern American diets are critically deficient in it, and that supplementation is the only practical way to get enough.",
        verbatim: "Calcium is essential for all organisms and is found in the cell walls of\nplants, all calcareous tissues, and mammalian bones. Calcium also helps\nregulate electrochemical functions in cells and activates several enzymes.\nCalcium is the fifth most abundant mineral element in the earth\u2019s crust and\nbiosphere.",
        page: null,
