        question: "What causes calcium deficiency or blocks calcium absorption?",
        answer_short: "Low vitamin D, low stomach acid, lactose intolerance, celiac disease, high-fat or low-protein diets, and phytates all reduce the calcium the body can absorb.",
        answer: "According to Wallach, causes of calcium deficiency include a lack of vitamin D and a deficiency of stomach acid \u2014 the low stomach acid (hypochlorhydria) itself often stemming from a salt-restricted diet. Lactose intolerance, celiac disease, high-fat diets, low protein intake, and high phytate consumption also lead to deficiency. In the same discussion he notes phytic acid in grain bran binds calcium into unabsorbable calcium phytate, oxalic acid in foods like spinach and rhubarb forms unabsorbable calcium oxalate, and dietary fiber itself prevents calcium absorption.",
        verbatim: "Causes of calcium deficiency include a lack of vitamin D. So does a\ndeficiency of stomach acid. Often the lack of stomach acid or\nhypochlorhydria results from a restricted NaCl (salt) intake. Lactose\nintolerance, celiac disease, high fat diet, and low protein intake and high\nphytate consumption can lead to calcium deficiency.",
