        facet: "stance",
        question: "What is the ideal calcium to phosphorus ratio?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says the ideal dietary calcium-to-phosphorus ratio is 2:1, which is impossible to hit from food alone because the American diet is loaded with phosphorus.",
        answer: "Wallach teaches that the ideal calcium-to-phosphorus (Ca:P) ratio in the daily diet should be 2:1, but that this is not possible by eating food alone. American soils and food are deficient in calcium while the diet is rich in phosphorus, which is in almost everything eaten. He drives the point home with an example: to balance a 16-ounce steak you would have to eat 25 pounds of broccoli \u2014 making calcium supplementation, plus cutting high-phosphorus items like colas, the only practical way to approach the 2:1 ideal.",
        verbatim: "Not only are our soils and food deficient in calcium, additionally the\nAmerican diet is rich in phosphorous, which is found in just about\neverything we eat. Ideally, the calcium to phosphorous (or Ca:P) ratio in our\ndaily diet should be 2:1. This ideal ratio is not possible by simply eating\nfood. You would have to eat 25 pounds of broccoli every time you ate a 16\noz. steak!",
        page: null,
