        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What causes Korsakoff's syndrome?",
        answer_short: "Korsakoff's syndrome \u2014 recent memory loss that mimics Alzheimer's \u2014 results from chronic alcoholism and vitamin B1 deficiency tied to excess simple carbohydrate and sugar.",
        answer: "Korsakoff's syndrome is a recent-memory loss that Wallach calls a 'false Alzheimer's disease,' characterized by an inability to record and store new memories \u2014 the patient can still perform tasks learned before onset but cannot learn the simplest new ones. It can result from a blow to the head or from chronic alcoholism together with vitamin B1 deficiency, associated with an excess intake of simple carbohydrate and sugar. Confabulation \u2014 producing imaginary experiences to fill in what cannot be recalled \u2014 is a consistent feature.",
        verbatim: "KORSAKOFF\u2019S SYNDROME (recent memory loss, false Alzheimer\u2019s\nDisease) is characterized by an inability to record and store new memory.\nThe patient can perform detailed tasks learned before onset but cannot learn\nthe simplest of new tasks. This type of \u201Camnesia\u201D can result from a blow to\nthe head or be the result of chronic alcoholism and vitamin B1 deficiency\nassociated with an excess intake of simple carbohydrate and sugar.",
