        question: "Why does it take years for vegans to become B12 deficient?",
        answer_short: "The body recycles B12 through an intestine-to-liver loop and stores it, so a B12 deficiency in vegans may not appear for five to ten years after giving up meat.",
        answer: "Vitamin B12 travels through an enterohepatic circulation, a recycling loop that pulls B12 back from bile and other intestinal secretions and returns it to the liver. This conservation is why B12 deficiency in vegans may not appear for five to ten years after they give up meat. On top of that, the body can store up to 2 mg of B12, releasing it slowly to the bone marrow as needed; any excess beyond storage capacity is simply shed in the urine, which Wallach dryly calls 'expensive urine.'",
        verbatim: "There is an enterohepatic (intestine direct to the\nliver) circulation of Vitamin B12 that recycles B12 from bile and other\nintestinal secretions which explains why B12 deficiency in vegans may not\nappear for five to ten years after giving up meat.\nThe maximum storage level of B12 is 2 mg, which is slowly released to the\nbone marrow as needed. Excess intake of B12 above the body\u2019s storage\ncapacity is shed in the urine (expensive urine).",
