        question: "How did Wallach discover that nitrates cause goiter?",
        answer_short: "A farmer's flock of lambs died of goiter despite iodine in their salt block; Wallach traced it to nitrates in drought-stressed corn, his first published paper.",
        answer: "Early in his veterinary career Wallach autopsied a flock of lambs that had died overnight with obvious goiter, their thyroid glands enlarged and their blood tinged chocolate. The farmer had dutifully provided iodine in a trace-mineral salt block, so iodine was not the problem. Wallach found the drought-stressed corn silage was heavy with nitrates, which act as a goitrogen that depresses thyroid activity and creates goiter even when iodine is present. The lambs had nitrate-induced hypothyroidism and could not control their body temperature on cool nights, and the case became his first peer-reviewed journal article.",
        verbatim: "Nitrates act as a goitrogen, a\nsubstance that depresses thyroid activity and creates a goiter, even when\niodine is present in the diet. All the lambs had nitrate-induced goiter and\nhypothyroidism. They died during the cool fall nights, because they\ncouldn\u2019t control their body temperature. This flock of lambs with nitrate-induced hypothyroidism became the subject of my first peer review,\nrefereed journal article. I was published!",
        page: null,
