        question: "How was germanium's healing potential discovered?",
        answer_short: "In 1950 Japanese chemist Dr. Kazuhiko Asai found germanium in fossilized plant life and linked healing herbs to their high germanium levels, while Russian researchers tied it to anti-cancer activity.",
        answer: "In 1950 the Japanese chemist Dr. Kazuhiko Asai found traces of germanium in fossilized plant life \u2014 the start of a long line of research into the element's healing role. Russian researchers soon attributed anti-cancer activity to germanium, and Asai went on to connect the healing reputation of certain herbs to their unusually high germanium content; many prized medicinal herbs turn out to be germanium 'accumulator' plants. Wallach frames germanium as a semiconductor-like element that helps move electrons inside cells and aids oxygen use, and credits it with stimulating immune defenders such as natural killer cells and interferon.",
        verbatim: "In 1950, Dr. Kazuhiko Asai, a Japanese chemist, found traces of germanium\nin fossilized plant life. Russian researchers quickly attributed anti-cancer\nactivity to germanium. Dr. Asai was able to connect the healing properties\nof certain herbs to relatively high levels of germanium. Many of these herbs\nare germanium accumulator plants.",
        page: null,
