        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is phosphorus?",
        answer_short: "Phosphorus is an extremely important essential mineral that Wallach says has more functions in the body than any other mineral, yet nutritionists ignore it because it is in nearly all foods.",
        answer: "Wallach calls phosphorus an extremely important essential mineral that gets little attention from nutritionists only because it is so widely available in food. It is a major structural mineral of the bones and teeth and, he says, has more functions in the human body than any other mineral: a vital constituent of nucleic acids (DNA), an activator of enzymes, and a driver of several steps of the adenosine triphosphate (ATP) energy cycle and red-blood-cell metabolism. Second only to calcium in abundance, it makes up about 22 percent of the body's total mineral content, and Wallach notes that a complete discussion of phosphorus would require a discussion of every metabolic function in the body.",
        verbatim: "Phosphorus is an extremely important essential mineral, however, it\ngets little or no attention from nutritionists because it is widely available in\nall foods. Phosphorus is a major structural mineral for bones and teeth. It\nhas more functions in the human than any other mineral including its role as\na vital constituent of nucleic acids, activating enzymes, and for several steps\nof the ATP energy cycle, RBC metabolism.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "dddl-3e-2011",
        topics: [
          "phosphorus",
          "minerals",
          "essential nutrients",
          "bone health",
          "energy"
        ]
