        facet: "sources",
        question: "Does phosphorus in grains block absorption of other minerals?",
        answer_short: "Yes \u2014 phosphorus in grains occurs as phytates, and phytic acid in unleavened bread binds calcium, iron, zinc and other minerals, lowering their absorption.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that phosphorus is present as phytates in cereals and whole grain flours. When bread is made from unleavened flours, the phytic acid complexes with calcium, iron, zinc and other minerals in the gut, further lowering their absorption rate. This is why phosphorus in grain form can work against the very minerals the body needs.",
        verbatim: "Phosphorus is\npresent as phytates in cereals and whole grain flours, therefore, if bread is\nmade from unleavened flours, the phytic acid will complex with Ca, Fe, Zn,\nand other minerals further lowering their absorption rate.",
