        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is Chloride?",
        answer_short: "Chloride is an essential mineral and one of the body's three key electrolytes; Wallach says its most important job is supplying the raw material your stomach uses to make hydrochloric acid.",
        answer: "In Wallach's framework, chloride is essential to every living species \u2014 it drives electrochemical and catalytic reactions and activates numerous enzymes. He groups it with sodium and potassium as one of three indispensable electrolytes that govern water balance, osmotic equilibrium, acid-base balance, and muscular function. Its single most important role, though, is serving as the basic raw material the stomach uses to manufacture hydrochloric acid (HCl) \u2014 the stomach acid that digests protein via pepsin, activates intrinsic factor for vitamin B12 absorption, and lets the body absorb minerals. Ordinary table salt (sodium chloride) is the universal source of chloride ions.",
        verbatim: "Chlorine is essential for all living species\u2019 electrochemical and catalytic\nfunctions, activates numerous enzymes, and is the basic raw material for\nour stomachs to make stomach acid (HCl) for protein digestion by pepsin,\nB12 absorption (intrinsic factor activation), and absorption of minerals.",
