        question: "What does the sweat test measure and is it really specific to cystic fibrosis?",
        answer_short: "It measures sodium, chloride, and potassium in sweat; Wallach says it is nonspecific, turning positive in 17 different diseases.",
        answer: "Wallach describes the sweat test as a nonspecific test that measures the amount of electrolytes\u2014sodium, chlorides, and potassium\u2014in sweat. Though originally thought to be a specific genetic indicator for cystic fibrosis, he says it is now known to be positive in 17 different diseases plus food allergies, starvation, kwashiorkor, and celiac disease. He attributes the mechanism to essential fatty acid deficiencies and the resulting lack of prostaglandins that control sweat electrolyte levels in the skin.",
        verbatim: "SWEAT TEST a nonspecific test that measures the amount of electrolytes\n(i.e. sodium, chlorides, and potassium) in sweat. Originally it was thought\nto be a specific genetic indicator for cystic fibrosis (CF). However, today it\nhas been found to be positive in 17 different diseases, food allergies,\nstarvation, kwashiorkor, celiac disease, etc. The mechanism is related to\nEFA deficiencies and thus the lack of certain prostaglandins (short-lived\nhormone-like substances) which control sweat electrolyte levels in the skin.",
        page: null,
