        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What does threonine do in the body?",
        answer_short: "In Wallach's function table, threonine's role is protein production\u2014it's a building block the body uses to manufacture its proteins, alongside valine, lysine, leucine, and isoleucine.",
        answer: "When Wallach lays out what each essential amino acid actually does, he assigns threonine a single, foundational role: protein production. It sits in the same group as valine, lysine, leucine, and isoleucine\u2014the amino acids whose listed job is simply building protein\u2014rather than the specialists like tryptophan (a precursor of niacin and serotonin) or phenylalanine (a precursor of thyroxin and epinephrine). In other words, threonine is raw material: the body strings it together with the other amino acids to manufacture the proteins that make up its tissues.",
        verbatim: "The classic essential amino acids are:\nAmino acid Function\nValine protein production\nLysine protein production\nThreonine protein production\nLeucine protein production\nIsoleucine protein production\nTryptophan precursor of niacin and serotonin\nPhenylalanine precursor of thyroxin and epinephrine\nMethionine formation of choline and creatine phosphate\nHistidine formation of histamine",
