        question: "What is oxygen and why does the body need it?",
        answer_short: "Oxygen is one of the three obvious raw materials every living thing needs to sustain life, and unlike food it is a 'non-variable' \u2014 the same everywhere on earth.",
        answer: "Wallach lists oxygen, water, and food as the obvious raw materials every living organism needs to sustain life, maintain and repair tissues and organs, reproduce, and support longevity. He draws a sharp line between them: clean oxygen and water, free of pathogens and pollution, are 'non-variables' \u2014 the same in China, Germany, Brazil, Nigeria, Canada, and all fifty U.S. states. Food, by contrast, is the variable, because all nourishment ultimately comes from the soil and depends on its mineral composition. In his framework oxygen is essentially a given; the real problem to solve is the mineral-depleted food supply.",
        verbatim: "Raw materials are required by all living organisms. The obvious raw\nmaterials to sustain life, maintain and repair tissues and organs, support\nreproduction, and support longevity, are oxygen, water, and food. Clean\noxygen and water, free of pathogens and pollution, are non-variables.\nOxygen and water are the same in China, Germany, Brazil, Nigeria,\nCanada, and all fifty states in the United States.",
