        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is Alzheimer's disease and what causes it, according to Wallach?",
        answer_short: "Wallach defines Alzheimer's as a physician-caused disease of progressive memory loss, first identified in 1979, caused by a cholesterol and fat deficiency -- and in his view not genetic.",
        answer: "Wallach defines Alzheimer's disease as a 'physician-caused' disease -- one he blames on the medical campaign against dietary cholesterol and saturated fat (what he calls 'cholesterol and saturated fat bashing'). He describes it as a progressive loss of memory, with difficulties in arousal and motor function appearing in the disease's later stages. He says it was identified in 1979 and holds that it results from a cholesterol and fat deficiency, not from genetics -- stating flatly that 'Alzheimer's disease is not genetic.' In his framing the brain needs cholesterol to rebuild myelin, so a diet stripped of cholesterol and fat starves it of that raw material.",
        verbatim: "ALZHEIMER\u2019S DISEASE appears to be a physician-caused disease\n(resulting from cholesterol and saturated fat bashing) in which those\nafflicted suffer from a progressive loss of memory. Difficulties with arousal\nand motor function appear in the later stages of the disease. Identified in\n1979, it appears to be due to a cholesterol and fat deficiency. Alzheimer\u2019s\ndisease is not genetic.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "dddl-3e-2011",
        topics: [
          "alzheimers",
          "memory-loss",
          "dementia",
          "brain-health",
          "what-is-alzheimers",
          "causes"
        ]
