        facet: "stance",
        question: "What does Wallach think of GMO foods?",
        answer_short: `Wallach calls genetically engineered crops "Frankenstein Food" and argues that manipulating genes without supplying the body's basic essential nutrients is a faulty, greedy premise.`,
        answer: 'Wallach calls the 1990s corporate drive to patent genetically engineered crops "Frankenstein Food," and lumps it together with patented enzymes, hormones, bacteria, and cloned animals and humans as a "heroic, if not greedy" effort by entrepreneurial scientists to further what he calls the "cult of allopathy." His core objection is that genetically manipulated living cells cannot develop, maintain themselves, reach their genetic potential for longevity, or perform without "the basic stuff of life" \u2014 the essential nutrients. He likens the whole enterprise to expecting a Mercedes to be driven full-speed from San Diego to Los Angeles without any oil or coolant: genetic potential without the raw nutritional materials to support it is, in his view, faulty in its premise.',
        verbatim: "The 90s drive to patent genetically engineered crops (\u201CFrankenstein Food\u201D),\nenzymes, hormones, bacteria, cloned animals (a* la Dolly the cloned lamb),\nand even humans (identical humans can now be cloned at the embryonic\nstage: \u201CThe Boys from Brazil\u201D are here!), has been a heroic, if not greedy, effort\nby our entrepreneurial scientists to further the cult of allopathy. But\nexpecting genetically manipulated living cells to develop, maintain\nthemselves, reach their genetic-potential for longevity, and perform without\nthe basic stuff of life",
        page: null,
        book_id: "dddl-3e-2011",
        topics: [
          "gmo",
          "genetic engineering",
          "frankenstein food",
          "biotechnology"
        ]
