        facet: "biography",
        question: "What did Dr. Wallach do at Emory University?",
        answer_short: "Wallach worked as a comparative pathologist at Emory University's Yerkes Regional Primate Research Center in Atlanta, a center administered by Emory and funded by the NIH and NASA, across the street from the CDC.",
        answer: "Dr. Wallach's connection to Emory University came through the Yerkes Regional Primate Research Center in Atlanta, Georgia, where he worked as a comparative (veterinary) pathologist after a four-year stint at the Brookfield Zoo. The primate center was administered by Emory University and funded by a variety of agencies including the NIH and NASA, and it sat directly across the street from the Centers for Disease Control \u2014 which Wallach describes as a dream position for a comparative pathologist. His job was to help the investigators understand everything about their primates, their diets, and their deaths, with nutritional deficiencies in monkeys, apes, and humans studied closely.",
        verbatim: "After a four year stint at the Brookfield Zoo, I wound up at the Yerkes\nRegional Primate Research Center in Atlanta, Georgia. The primate center\nwas administered by Emory University and funded by a variety of agencies\nincluding NIH and NASA. Physically the center was located across the\nstreet from the Centers for Disease Control, a dream position for a\ncomparative pathologist.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "dddl-3e-2011",
