        facet: "basics",
        question: "What are the different types of dementia?",
        answer_short: "Wallach frames dementia as at least four separate nutritional diseases -- vascular dementia, Korsakoff's syndrome, Wernicke-Korsakoff's syndrome, and Alzheimer's -- and says a person can have some degree of two, three, or all four at the same time.",
        answer: `Wallach frames dementia not as one disease but as a minimum of four distinct nutritional diseases, and stresses that a person can have some degree of two, three, or all four at the same time. (1) Vascular dementia -- the brain's arteries become obstructed by atherosclerosis/arteriosclerosis from inflammation of the arterial lining, driven by low dietary antioxidants plus fried foods, processed meats, oils, and gluten. (2) Korsakoff's syndrome -- part of the beriberi family (thiamine/vitamin B1 deficiency), reversible by removing sugar, fried foods, processed meats, oils, and gluten and supplementing all 90 essential nutrients with special emphasis on thiamine. (3) Wernicke-Korsakoff's syndrome -- Korsakoff's syndrome plus multiple sclerosis (MS) at the same time; addressed by treating both, adding 6-8 eggs a day to give the brain raw material to rebuild myelin, and emphasizing antioxidants including selenium. (4) Alzheimer's disease -- which Wallach calls a 'physician-caused' disease, produced by lowering the patient's cholesterol and saturated fat, prescribing statin drugs, and telling them to avoid vitamin and mineral supplements. In Wallach's words: "Dementia manifests itself as a minimum of four different diseases. Sometimes, individuals will have some degree of two, three, or all four of the classic dementias at the same time."`,
        verbatim: "Dementia manifests itself as a minimum of four different diseases. Sometimes,\nindividuals will have some degree of two, three, or all four of the classic\ndementias at the same time",
