        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "What does hydrogen do in the body?",
        answer_short: "Wallach explains hydrogen is the major constituent of water (about 70% of the body) and all organic molecules, and that the body's acid-base balance is really the regulation of hydrogen-ion (H+) levels.",
        answer: "Hydrogen is an essential structural element: it is the major constituent of water (about 70 percent of the human body is water) and of all organic molecules, and Wallach frames the body's acid-base balance as, in fact, the regulation of hydrogen-ion (H+) levels in cellular and extracellular fluids. Healthy blood pH ranges from 7.36 to 7.44; below 7.30 is acidosis and above 7.44 is alkalosis, while values below 6.8 or above 7.8 are rapidly fatal. The body controls hydrogen-ion concentration by dilution, buffering (reacting in fractions of a second), respiration (minutes) and the kidney (up to hours or days). Large amounts of hydrogen ions are generated or retained in disease states such as emphysema, diabetes, anxiety, chloride loss, cystic fibrosis and Addison's disease. He notes a practical corollary: bladder infections (cystitis) can often be controlled by acidifying the urine with unsweetened cranberry juice.",
        verbatim: "Hydrogen functions as a major constituent of water (70% of the human body\nis water) and all organic molecules. The regulation of the acid-base balance in the\nhuman body is in fact the regulation of the hydrogen ion (H+) levels of cellular\nand extracellular fluids.",
