        answer: "Potassium is essential to all organisms and is the major positively-charged ion (cation) inside cells, driving electrochemical and enzyme functions; it makes up about five percent of the body's total mineral content. With sodium, the other main electrolyte, potassium maintains normal water balance, osmotic equilibrium and acid-base balance, and works with calcium to regulate neuromuscular activity. It is easily absorbed but barely stored -- about 90 percent of ingested potassium is excreted in the urine -- so Wallach states a significant daily intake of 5,000 milligrams is required. Chronic potassium deficiency shows as muscular weakness and mental apathy, and its most serious event is hypokalemic (low-potassium) cardiac failure. Diuretics (both natural and prescribed), sweating from colds, flu or exercise, vomiting and diarrhea all increase the loss of potassium and other minerals.",
