        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is Vitamin C?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that most mammals make in their own bodies, but humans cannot -- so we must get it from food or supplements every day.",
        answer: "Vitamin C, also called ascorbic acid, is a water-soluble antioxidant. Wallach explains that many mammals can synthesize their own vitamin C internally, but humans -- like guinea pigs -- cannot, so people must obtain it from the diet. It belongs to the water-soluble vitamin class (alongside the B-complex) and serves as a required cofactor for building collagen -- the protein behind connective tissue, cartilage, bones, teeth, and blood vessels. Its classic role is preventing and curing scurvy, the deficiency disease that plagued sailors and explorers for centuries.",
