        question: "Who discovered the cure for scurvy?",
        answer_short: "Scottish naval surgeon James Lind proved in 1753 that lemons and oranges cured scurvy, in one of the first controlled experiments.",
        answer: "Wallach recounts that James Lind, a Scottish naval surgeon, eliminated scurvy in the British navy after studying the legend of a marooned sailor's self-cure. In 1753, as recorded in his 'A Treatise on Scurvy,' Lind divided scurvy-stricken sailors into six groups and fed each different foods; only the sailors given lemons and oranges recovered rapidly and completely. Because Lind did not prescribe a specific treatment from his results, it took another forty-two years before the British navy adopted his findings.",
        verbatim: "James Lind, a Scottish naval surgeon, finally eliminated scurvy in the British\nnavy after researching the legend of Zoe and his miraculous self-cure. In 1753,\nas he recorded in his A Treatise on Scurvy, Lind divided sailors with scurvy into\nsix separate groups. Each group was provided with different foods, and only the\nsailors who consumed lemons and oranges recovered rapidly and completely.\nLind did not prescribe a specific treatment as a result of his study, so it took\nanother forty-two years before the British navy adopted his recommendations.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
        topics: [
          "vitamin c",
          "scurvy",
          "history",
          "james lind"
        ]
