        answer: "Vitamin A is a fat-soluble vitamin, first isolated in 1913, that exists in several active forms \u2014 retinol, retinoic acid, and retinyl esters \u2014 plus precursors. Beta-carotene is the fat-soluble precursor the body converts into vitamin A. Wallach teaches that it is required to maintain vision and night vision, healthy skin and mucous membranes, and bones and teeth, and that it has been shown to reduce the risk of epithelial cancers. Because it is fat-soluble it is stored mainly in the liver and absorbed along with dietary fats, which is why a low-fat diet or any condition that blocks fat absorption can leave you short of it.",
        verbatim: "This vitamin, isolated in 1913, which has many active forms including, retinol,\nretinoic acid and retinyl esters and precursors.",
