        facet: "discovery",
        question: "How was vitamin A discovered?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin A was discovered in 1913 when two research groups found that animals fed only lard developed a deficiency that was corrected by adding butter, egg yolk, and cod liver oil.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that vitamin A was discovered through experiment rather than mere observation, in 1913. Two groups working independently, Osborne and Mendel, and McCollum and Davis, reported that animals fed artificial diets using lard as their only source of fat developed a nutritional deficiency. That deficiency could be corrected by adding foods such as butter, egg yolk, and cod liver oil to the diet, revealing the missing factor now known as vitamin A.",
        verbatim: "Experimental, rather than observational evidence, led to the discovery of\nvitamin A in 1913. Two groups (Osborne and Mendel; McCollum and Davis)\nindependently reported that animals fed on artificial diets with lard as a\nsole source of fat developed a nutritional deficiency that could be corrected\nby the addition of foods such as butter, egg yolk, and cod liver oil to the\ndiet.",
