        question: "What is iron?",
        answer_short: "Iron is an essential mineral found in nearly every living thing; in the body it sits at the heart of hemoglobin, the protein that carries oxygen in the blood, and a healthy adult holds about 3 to 5 grams of it.",
        answer: "Iron is a necessary element present in nearly all living organisms, and one of the 60 essential minerals Wallach says the body must obtain from diet or supplements. It works through iron-containing enzymes and proteins, many of them built around heme groups, that drive biological oxidation and oxygen transport, including hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochrome, and catalase. A healthy adult carries 3 to 5 grams of total iron, a newborn nearly double that per kilogram of body weight; roughly 60 to 70 percent is functional iron doing active work and 30 to 40 percent is held in storage. In Wallach's framework iron is the metal that lets blood carry oxygen, and running short of it, a deficiency he treats as common given how many everyday conditions drain it, drives fatigue, anemia, poor memory, and the strange cravings of pica.",
