        answer: "Wallach recounts that the taming of fire brought the mastery of metals, and iron was the primary key of that transition, lifting human life from a brutish hunter-gatherer existence toward civilization. This early history gave iron a special, almost supernatural quality, reinforced by the fact that the first purified iron was extracted from meteors that fell from the heavens. For that reason the ancient Egyptians called iron 'the metal from the sky,' and the Aztecs called it 'the gift of heaven.'",
        verbatim: "The \u201Ctaming\u201D of fire by man brought the \u201Cmastery\u201D of metals that eventually\nled to human progress and civilization. Iron was the primary key of this transition,\n\nwhich raised the quality of human life from a brutish animal-like hunter gatherer\n\nexistence to civilization. This early history of the ferrous metal was enough to\ngive iron a special supernatural quality. Additionally, the first purified iron was\nextracted from meteors that fell from the heavens.\n\nThe ancient Egyptians referred to iron as \u201Cthe metal from the sky,\u201D and the\nAztecs referred to iron as \u201Cthe gift of heaven.\u201D",
