        question: "How much calcium is in the human body and where is it stored?",
        answer_short: "Calcium is the most abundant mineral in the body\u2014about 1,200 g in men and 1,000 g in women\u2014with 99 percent of it stored in the bones and teeth.",
        answer: "Wallach states that calcium is the most abundant mineral in the human body: the average man carries about 1,200 grams (roughly 3 pounds) and the average woman about 1,000 grams (roughly 2 pounds), around two percent of body weight and up to 39 percent of the body's total mineral reserve. Ninety-nine percent of that calcium is held in the bones and teeth. The remaining one percent circulates in blood, extracellular fluid, and cells, where it acts as a cofactor and activator for numerous chemical, DNA, and enzymatic reactions.",
        verbatim: "Calcium is the most abundant mineral in the human body. The average male\nhas 1,200 grams (approximately 3 pounds) and the average female has 1,000\ngrams (approximately 2 pounds), which makes up about two percent of the\nhuman body weight (water makes up 65 to 75%) and up to 39 percent of the\ntotal mineral reserves of the human body (ash). Ninety-nine percent of the total\nbody calcium is found in the bones and teeth, and the remaining one percent is.\nfound in the blood, extracellular fluid, and within cells where it is a cofactor and\nactivator for numerous chemical, DNA, and enzymatic reactions.",
