        answer: "Working with zoo reptiles, Wallach found that tortoises and iguanas fed fruit, eggs, meat, and lettuce with no added calcium universally developed kidney stones. The calcium in those stones, he determined, came from the animals' own bones, skeletons, and shells, dissolved by raging osteoporosis when the diet lacked calcium. Because the same phenomenon was already well documented in domestic animals, he could cite refereed references showing kidney stones develop from too little dietary calcium, not too much.",
