        question: "What is Vitamin B1?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin B1 (thiamine) is a water-soluble B-complex vitamin and an essential cofactor for energy production and the metabolism of carbohydrates.",
        answer: "Vitamin B1, also called thiamine, is one of the water-soluble vitamins of the B-complex. In Wallach's framework it is essential throughout life for tissue respiration and energy production: thiamine combines with phosphorus to form the coenzyme thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), which drives the oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate at the entrance to the Krebs cycle. Because carbohydrate metabolism depends on thiamine, the daily requirement rises with the amount of dietary sugar and starch. Discovered in 1897 and isolated in 1911, thiamine is also the vitamin from which the word 'vitamin' itself was coined.",
        verbatim: "This water soluble vitamin was discovered in 1897 and isolated in 1911.\nIt is a cofactor required for energy production and optimal metabolism of\ncarbohydrates.",
