        also_about: [],
        facet: "etymology",
        question: "Why are vitamins called vitamins?",
        answer_short: "The word 'vitamin' traces to thiamine: Funk's crystalline anti-beriberi substance was a basic (vital) amine, which he named a 'vital amine' \u2014 later shortened to vitamin.",
        answer: "The very word 'vitamin' traces back to thiamine (vitamin B1). Wallach explains that Funk produced a crystalline substance from rice polishings and yeast that could prevent and cure experimentally produced beriberi. Because the substance was a basic (high-pH) nitrogen compound \u2014 an amine essential to life \u2014 Funk called it a 'vit- (vital) amine.' The term was then accepted as a broad label for dietary substances deemed to be dietary essentials.",
        verbatim: "Some years later, Funk produced a crystalline substance\nfrom rice polishings and yeast that was effective in the prevention and cure\nof experimentally produced beriberi. The substance contained a high pH (basic)\nnitrogen (amine), so Funk called it a vit- (vital) amine. The term was accepted as a\nbroad label for dietary substances that were deemed to be dietary essentials.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
        topics: [
          "etymology",
          "vitamins",
          "history",
          "discovery"
        ]
