        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is niacin (vitamin B3)?",
        answer_short: "Niacin, or vitamin B3, is a water-soluble B vitamin the body turns into the coenzymes NAD and NADP to release energy from food; a severe deficiency causes pellagra.",
        answer: "Niacin \u2014 vitamin B3 \u2014 is a water-soluble B vitamin that Wallach also names nicotinic acid, nicotinamide, or niacinamide. Isolated in 1900, it works in the body as part of two coenzymes, NAD and NADP (the pyridine nucleotides), which cells use to release energy from carbohydrates, fats, and proteins. The body can also make its own niacin from the amino acid tryptophan. A severe niacin deficiency produces pellagra \u2014 the classic 'three Ds' of dermatitis, dementia, and diarrhea \u2014 and Wallach uses niacin at doses far above the RDA for problems ranging from high cholesterol to schizophrenia.",
        verbatim: "Vitamin B,, isolated in 1900, functions in humans as a part of the coenzymes\nnicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide\nphosphate (NADP), known as the pyridine nucleotides.",
        page: null,
