        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "What does vitamin B3 (niacin) actually do in the body?",
        answer_short: "Niacin forms the coenzymes NAD and NADP, which cells use to release energy from carbohydrates, fats, and proteins.",
        answer: "Niacin (also called nicotinic acid, nicotinamide, or niacinamide) was isolated in 1900 and works as part of two coenzymes: nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADP), together known as the pyridine nucleotides. These coenzymes interact with the cell's respiratory enzymes and are essential to the oxidation-reduction reactions that release energy from carbohydrates, fats, and proteins. In their reduced forms they become NADH and NADPH, and NAD is also required for glycogen synthesis.",
        verbatim: "Vitamin B, (niacin, nicotinic acid, nicotinamide, niacinamide) function:\n\nVitamin B,, isolated in 1900, functions in humans as a part of the coenzymes\nnicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide\nphosphate (NADP), known as the pyridine nucleotides. These coenzymes interact\nwith cellular respiratory enzymes. They are essential to the oxidation-reduction\nreactions in the release of energy from carbohydrates, fats and proteins. These\ncoenzymes in their reduced forms are NADH and NADPH. In addition NAD is\nrequired for glycogen synthesis.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
