        facet: "warning",
        question: "What are the side effects of too much niacin?",
        answer_short: "Overdosing on nicotinic acid can cause hot skin flushes, ulcers, liver disease, elevated blood sugar and uric acid, cardiac arrhythmia, and dry, itchy skin.",
        answer: "Wallach draws a sharp line: it is specifically the nicotinic-acid form of niacin (vitamin B3) that can cause overdose trouble. Too much brings on the hot skin 'flush' niacin is famous for, plus ulcers, liver disease, raised blood sugar and uric acid, cardiac arrhythmia, and dry, itchy skin. He points out the same high pharmacological doses (three to nine grams a day) are also what doctors use deliberately to lower cholesterol.",
        verbatim: "Signs of overdose: Vitamin B; (nicotinic acid only) overdose can result in hot\nflushes of the skin, ulcers, liver disease, elevated blood sugar and uric acid, cardiac\narrhythmia, and dry, itchy skin.",
