        facet: "history",
        question: "Where did pellagra come from and why did it appear in Europe?",
        answer_short: "Pellagra was unknown in Europe until Columbus brought corn from the New World; peasants who became dependent on corn developed the disease.",
        answer: "Wallach traces pellagra to the arrival of corn: the disease was unknown in Europe until Christopher Columbus brought the first corn seeds back from the New World. European peasants embraced corn as an easy 'miracle crop' and became dependent on it, and whole villages and towns then came down with what Wallach calls the 'four Ds' \u2014 dermatitis (skin disease), dementia, diarrhea, and death. That was pellagra.",
        verbatim: 'Pellagra is considered a \u201Cnew disease\u201D in the civilized world, as it was unknown\nin Europe until Christopher Columbus delivered the first corn seeds from the\n\u201CNew World" to Europe. The European peasant looked upon corn as an easy to\nproduce miracle crop; it spread quickly as a food crop and the peasants became\ndependent on corn.\n\nCorn was the beginning of the end for millions of people. Complete villages\nand towns came down with a plague of skin disease (dermatitis), dementia,\ndiarthea, and death (the four Ds\u2014pellagra had arrived.)',
