        answer_short: "Scientists found pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) in the 1930s by accident, while searching for a nutrient that would make yeast grow.",
        answer: "Pantothenic acid, vitamin B5, was discovered in the 1930s essentially by accident. The scientists were not looking for the cause, prevention, or cure of any particular disease; they were searching for a nutrient that would stimulate the growth of yeast. Along the way they found a substance that supported yeast growth and that, when missing from animal diets, produced deficiency diseases. Wallach notes the vitamin was formally isolated in 1940.",
