        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is folate?",
        answer_short: "Folate \u2014 vitamin B-9, also called folic acid or folacin \u2014 is an essential B vitamin the body needs to build DNA, RNA, and red blood cells. Wallach calls its deficiency perhaps the most common vitamin deficiency in humans.",
        answer: "Folate, known as vitamin B-9 and also called folic acid or folacin, is one of the essential B vitamins. Wallach explains that it was first identified in 1946 and is required for the synthesis of DNA and RNA and the formation of erythrocytes (red blood cells). Metabolically it acts as a coenzyme that shuttles single carbon units during amino acid metabolism and nucleic acid synthesis, working hand-in-hand with vitamin B12 \u2014 so a shortage of either vitamin produces the same megaloblastic, macrocytic anemia. Because folate builds the genetic material of every dividing cell, Wallach ties its deficiency to anemia, birth defects such as spina bifida, and a long list of other problems, and considers it perhaps the most common vitamin deficiency in humans.",
