        question: "Is mad cow disease caused by copper deficiency?",
        answer_short: "Wallach argues that mad cow disease (BSE) and CJD are copper-deficiency diseases, not prion infections, pointing to identical brain lesions and copper-poor soils where cases cluster.",
        answer: "Wallach contends that BSE (mad cow disease) in cattle and Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease in humans are copper-deficiency diseases rather than infectious prion diseases. He points out that the brain lesions in sheep with enzootic ataxia, a known copper-deficiency disease, are identical to the lesions in cattle with BSE and humans with CJD, and that cases of BSE and CJD are typically diagnosed in geographic areas where the soils are copper deficient. He also notes that attempts to transmit the disease by injecting prions have failed to satisfy Koch's postulates.",
        verbatim: "It is obvious that the brain lesions in sheep afflicted with enzootic ataxia,\na known copper-deficiency disease, are identical to the brain lesions in cattle\nafflicted with BSE and also to the brain lesions in humans afflicted with CJD.\nThe preponderance of the cases of BSE in cattle and CJD in humans are typically\ndiagnosed in geographical areas where the soils are copper deficient.",
