        answer_short: "Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant vitamin \u2014 actually a family of compounds called tocopherols \u2014 that protects cell membranes and red blood cells from oxidative damage.",
        answer: "Wallach describes vitamin E as a fat-soluble vitamin, first isolated in 1922, that is not a single molecule but a family of related compounds called tocopherols (specifically the alpha-tocopherols). Its core job is antioxidant protection: it shields cell membranes from oxidative inflammation and keeps red blood cells from rupturing (lysis). Working alongside the trace mineral selenium it also lowers the risk of certain cancers, slows the aging process, and helps preserve the length of telomeres. This antioxidant role is the foundation for its wider benefits across the heart, brain, muscles, and reproduction.",
