        facet: "discovery",
        question: "How was vitamin E discovered?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin E was recognized in 1922 when female rats were found to need an unknown dietary factor to carry pregnancies to term without miscarrying.",
        answer: "Wallach recounts that vitamin E's existence was first recognized in 1922, when researchers realized female rats required a previously unknown dietary factor to maintain their pregnancies. Deficient females could still ovulate and conceive normally, but at some point in the pregnancy they would suffer a spontaneous miscarriage. Lesions in the testes of deficient males were also reported, tying the new factor to fertility in both sexes.",
        verbatim: "The existence of vitamin E was first recognized in 1922 when it was learned\nthat female rats required a previously unknown dietary factor to maintain\npregnancies. Deficient females would ovulate and conceive properly; however,\nat some point in the pregnancy a spontaneous miscarriage would occur;\nadditionally, lesions in the male's testes were reported.",
        page: null,
