        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is Vitamin K?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin your liver needs to make prothrombin and other blood-clotting proteins and to lay calcium down properly in bone \u2014 so it governs both normal clotting and bone strength.",
        answer: "Vitamin K is one of the fat-soluble vitamins in Wallach's essential-nutrient framework. Isolated in 1939, it is required by the liver to produce prothrombin and at least five other proteins (clotting factors VII, IX and X and proteins C and S), so the blood can clot normally. It also drives the proper deposition of calcium into bone \u2014 through the vitamin K-dependent protein osteocalcin \u2014 which is why Wallach calls it as essential to healthy bone as vitamin D. It comes in two forms: K1 (phylloquinone) from green plants and K2 (menaquinones) made by friendly bacteria in the colon, which supply roughly half of the body's requirement.",
        verbatim: "This vitamin, isolated in 1939, is a fat soluble vitamin that is required by the liver\nfor the production of prothrombin and at least five other proteins (factors VII, IX,\nand X, and proteins C and S) and other biologically active substances essential\nfor proper blood clotting and for the proper deposition of calcium in bones.",
        page: null,
