        facet: "warning",
        question: "Can you take too much vitamin K?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says overdose signs include jaundice in newborns, and that injected phylloquinone or large doses of synthetic menadione can cause serious reactions and even death.",
        answer: "Wallach describes signs of vitamin K overdose: jaundice (kernicterus) in newborn infants, and \u2014 with rapid intravenous injection of phylloquinone in adults \u2014 flushing, shortness of breath, chest pain, and death. Large doses of the synthetic form menadione (not phylloquinone) given to animals produced hemolytic anemia, polycythemia (too many red blood cells), an enlarged spleen, kidney and liver damage, and death. He draws a clear line between the natural form and the greater toxicity of synthetic menadione.",
        verbatim: "Signs of overdose: Symptoms include jaundice (kernicterus) in newborn\ninfants; rapid intravenous injections of phylloquinone in adult humans can\nproduce flushing, dyspnea, chest pain, and death. Large doses of menadione\n(not phylloquinone) administered to animals has produced hemolytic anemia,\npolycythemia (too many red blood cells), splenomegaly, kidney and liver damage\n(hyperbilirubinemia) and death.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
