        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does inositol work in the body?",
        answer_short: "Myo-inositol triphosphate acts as a second messenger that mobilizes intracellular calcium in response to hormonal signals, and inositol is a building block of cell membranes.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that myo-inositol is believed to be an essential nutrient because myo-inositol triphosphate is a second messenger for receptor-mediated hormonal stimuli that mobilize intracellular calcium. It also appears to have a lipotrophic (fat-moving) function stemming from its role as a substrate for building phosphatidyl inositol and polyphosphoinositides, which are essential to the structure of cell membranes.",
        verbatim: "Myo-inositol is believed to be\nan essential nutrient because myo-inositol triphosphate is a second messenger\nfor receptor-mediated hormonal stimuli for mobilizing intracellular calcium.\nAdditionally, myo-inositol appears to have a lipotrophic function that may\noriginate from its vital role as a substrate for the biosynthesis of phosphatidyl\n\ninositol and polyphos-hoinositides, which are essential for the structure of\nbiomembranes.",
        page: null,
