        facet: "basics",
        question: "What are flavonoids?",
        answer_short: "Flavonoids are biologically active plant pigments \u2014 the yellow, red, and blue colors in flowers \u2014 that come in three classes and that Wallach argues are essential nutrients.",
        answer: "Flavonoids (also called bioflavonoids) are a diverse group of biologically active carbon compounds, and they are the plant pigments responsible for the yellow, red, and blue colors of flowers, colors that attract pollinating insects. Inside plants they also act as chemical and physiological messengers and as cell-cycle inhibitors. Wallach divides them into three classes \u2014 flavonoids (bioflavonoids), isoflavonoids, and neoflavonoids \u2014 and holds that there is more than enough evidence to treat them as essential nutrients.",
        verbatim: "Flavonoids are widely associated with plants. Flavonoids are plant pigments\nthat are found in flowers as yellow, red and blue petals, these colors are\nattractants for pollinating insects and animals indirectly associated with the\nannual sexual cycle of plants. In plants, bioflavonoids also act as chemical\nmessengers, physiological messengers, and cell cycle inhibitors.",
