        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "Why is methionine the 'start' amino acid in protein synthesis?",
        answer_short: "Methionine's codon, AUG, doubles as the cell's main 'Start' signal for building a protein, so nearly every protein begins with a methionine.",
        answer: "Methionine is encoded by a single codon, AUG \u2014 and AUG is also the most common 'Start' signal in eukaryotic cells. When it sits within a Kozak consensus sequence, AUG tells a ribosome to begin translating a protein from messenger RNA (mRNA). Because that start signal is methionine's own codon, protein synthesis characteristically begins with a methionine residue, giving this amino acid a foundational role in assembling the body's proteins.",
        verbatim: "The AUG codon is the most common eukaryote \u201CStart\u201D\nmessage for a ribosome that signals the initiation of protein translation from\nmRNA when the AUG codon is in a Kozak consensus sequence.",
        page: null,
