        facet: "stance",
        question: "Is phenylalanine an essential amino acid?",
        answer_short: "Yes\u2014Wallach lists phenylalanine among the nine classic essential amino acids the body cannot make on its own and must be supplied daily from the diet to prevent deficiency and disease.",
        answer: "Yes. Phenylalanine is one of the nine amino acids Wallach lists as classically 'essential': histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and valine. 'Essential' means the human body cannot synthesize these amino acids on its own, so they have to be consumed every day; without them, Wallach warns, deficiencies and their disease consequences follow. He also argues that three more amino acids\u2014arginine, taurine, and tyrosine\u2014should be treated as essential, because their long-term deficiency causes specific diseases too.",
        verbatim: "There are nine amino acids that are classically considered to be \u201Cessential\u201D\namino acids: Histidine, Isoleucine, leucine, Lysine, Methionine, Phenylalanine,\nThreonine, Tryptophan and Valine. These amino acids can\u2019t be synthesized\nby humans and must be consumed on a daily basis to prevent deficiencies\nand disease consequences.",
        page: null,
