        id: "WAL-CLM-EPIGEN-000364",
        subject: "phenylalanine",
        also_about: [],
        facet: "stance",
        question: "Is phenylketonuria (PKU) really a genetic disease?",
        answer_short: "Wallach calls PKU\u2014the inability to metabolize phenylalanine\u2014'falsely believed' to be a genetically transmitted disease, and notes its symptoms are prevented simply by avoiding phenylalanine.",
        answer: "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an inborn error of metabolism in which the body cannot properly break down phenylalanine. Wallach takes the pointed position that PKU is 'falsely believed to be a genetically-transmitted disease.' Whatever its cause, he notes the practical management is the same: avoiding phenylalanine in the diet prevents the symptoms of PKU. This is why phenylalanine-containing products such as the sweetener aspartame carry warnings for people with the condition.",
        verbatim: "The inborn error of metabolism known as phenylketonuria (PKU), falsely\nbelieved to be a genetically-transmitted disease, is the inability to metabolize\nphenylalanine. Avoidance of phenylalanine prevents the symptoms of PKU.",
        page: null,
