        facet: "warning",
        question: "Can large doses of phenylalanine lower serotonin?",
        answer_short: "Yes\u2014phenylalanine shares the same brain-entry transport channel as tryptophan, so in large doses it can crowd tryptophan out and interfere with serotonin production.",
        answer: "Phenylalanine uses the same active transport channel as tryptophan to cross the blood-brain barrier\u2014the protective filter that controls what enters the brain. Because the two amino acids compete for that same doorway, Wallach notes that phenylalanine in large doses will interfere with the production of serotonin, the mood- and sleep-related brain chemical the body makes from tryptophan. In effect, loading up on phenylalanine can push tryptophan out of the brain and blunt serotonin synthesis.",
        verbatim: "Phenylalanine employs the same active transport channel as tryptophan to cross\nthe blood-brain barrier, in large doses it will interfere with the production of\nserotonin.",
