        id: "WAL-CLM-EPIGEN-000369",
        subject: "tyrosine",
        also_about: [],
        facet: "etymology",
        question: "Where does the word tyrosine come from?",
        answer_short: "The name tyrosine comes from the Greek word for cheese, because the amino acid was first isolated from cheese in 1846 by the German chemist Justus von Liebig.",
        answer: "The word 'tyrosine' comes from the Greek word for cheese. Wallach explains this is because the amino acid was first isolated from cheese, in 1846, by the German chemist Justus von Liebig. The name reflects where scientists first found the molecule rather than any special property of it -- a small piece of history that ties this thyroid- and brain-supporting amino acid back to a wheel of aged cheese.",
        verbatim: "The origin of the word \u201Ctyrosine\u201D is the\nGreek word for cheese, as it was first isolated in 1846 from cheese by the German\nchemist Justus von Liebig.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
        topics: [
          "amino acids",
          "science history"
        ]
