        answer: "In mammals, lysine is broken down to produce acetyl-CoA, a key molecule that feeds the body's central energy-generating pathways. Wallach notes that this breakdown begins with an initial transamination, a reaction in which lysine hands off its nitrogen-containing amino group to alpha-ketoglutarate.",
        verbatim: "Lysine in mammals is metabolized to produce acetyl-CoA through an initial\ntransamination with alpha ketogluterate.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "epigenetics",
        topics: [
          "metabolism",
          "energy",
          "amino-acids"
        ]
