        question: "What is the 'lysine contingency' in Jurassic Park?",
        answer_short: "Wallach recounts how the film Jurassic Park gave its cloned dinosaurs a 'lysine contingency', engineering them so they couldn't make their own lysine, keeping them dependent on lysine supplements and unable to survive outside the park.",
        answer: "To illustrate how vital an essential amino acid is, Wallach points to the 1993 film Jurassic Park (from Michael Crichton's 1990 novel), in which dinosaurs are recreated by DNA engineering. In the story the dinosaurs are deliberately altered so they cannot produce their own lysine, the so-called 'lysine contingency', which would keep them from surviving outside the park and force them to depend on lysine supplements provided by the park's supplement program. The plot device works precisely because lysine is essential: an animal that cannot make it and is not fed it will not survive.",
