        also_about: [],
        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What kind of amino acid is leucine and what proteins is it part of?",
        answer_short: "Leucine is a branched-chain, hydrophobic alpha amino acid encoded by six codons; Wallach says it forms a significant part of the ferritin and astacin protein subunits and a class of 'buffer' proteins.",
        answer: "Leucine (abbreviated Leu) is a branched-chain alpha amino acid that Wallach classifies as hydrophobic, meaning it avoids water and tends to tuck into the interior of proteins. It is unusual in being specified by six different genetic codons (UUA, UUG, CUU, CUC, CUA, and CUG), more than most amino acids, and it makes up a significant part of the subunits of ferritin and astacin, as well as a class of so-called 'buffer' proteins. That structural profile is part of why leucine is woven throughout the body's core protein machinery rather than serving one narrow role.",
        verbatim: "Leucine (Leu) is a branched chain alpha amino acid that is classified as a\nhydrophobic amino acid. It has six codons (UUA, UUG, CUU, CUC, CUA, and CUG)\nand is a significant part of the subunits of ferritin, astacin, and a class of \u201Cbuffer\u201D\nproteins.",
        page: null,
