        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "Where in the body does leucine work?",
        answer_short: "Leucine has major functions in the liver, fat (adipose) tissue, and muscle; in muscle and fat it is used to synthesize sterols, and its combined use there is greater than in the liver.",
        answer: "Wallach places leucine's major functions in three tissues: the liver, the adipose (fat) tissue, and muscle. In both muscle and adipose tissue leucine is used to synthesize sterols\u2014the fat-like building blocks that include cholesterol and steroid compounds\u2014and he notes that leucine's combined use across muscle and fat is actually larger than the amount used by the liver. So while the liver is the usual hub of amino-acid metabolism, for leucine the muscle and fat tissues do the heavier work.",
        verbatim: "Leucine has major functions in the liver, adipose tissue, and muscle. In muscle\nand adipose tissue, leucine is employed to synthesize sterols and its combined use\nin these tissues is larger than that used by the liver.",
