        facet: "big_question",
        question: "What does valine have to do with sickle cell disease?",
        answer_short: "In sickle-cell disease valine takes the place of glutamic acid in hemoglobin; because valine repels water while glutamic acid attracts it, the hemoglobin becomes more likely to clump together.",
        answer: "Wallach points to valine's role in sickle-cell disease as an example of how a single amino-acid substitution changes a protein. In this condition, valine substitutes for glutamic acid within hemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying protein of red blood cells. Because glutamic acid is hydrophilic (water-loving) and valine is hydrophobic (water-repelling), swapping one for the other alters the protein's behavior in water and makes the hemoglobin more likely to aggregate\u2014to clump together.",
        verbatim: "In sickle-cell disease, valine can substitute for the hydrophilic amino acid\nglutamic acid in hemoglobin. Valine is hydrophobic; therefore the hemoglobin is\nmore likely to aggregate.",
