        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does histidine work in enzymes?",
        answer_short: "Histidine's imidazole side chain binds metals and sits in enzyme catalytic sites, where it pulls a proton off serine, threonine, or cysteine to switch them on as nucleophiles.",
        answer: "Histidine's chemistry comes from its imidazole side chain. That ring makes histidine a coordinating ligand of metalloproteins -- it binds and holds metal atoms -- and places it at the catalytic sites of certain enzymes. In a catalytic triad, the basic nitrogen of histidine abstracts (pulls away) a proton from a nearby serine, threonine, or cysteine, activating that residue as a nucleophile so it can attack and break chemical bonds. The same imidazole also lets histidine act as a proton shuttle, moving hydrogen ions quickly where they are needed.",
        verbatim: "The imidazole sidechain of histidine is a coordinating ligand of\nmetalloproteins and is a part of catalytic sites in certain enzymes. In catalytic\ntriads, the basic nitrogen of histidine is used to abstract a proton from serine,\nthreonine, or cysteine to activate it as a nucleophile.",
