        answer_short: "Yes\u2014Wallach lists threonine among the nine classic essential amino acids the body can't make itself, so it must be eaten every day to prevent deficiency diseases.",
        answer: `Wallach places threonine on the classic list of nine "essential" amino acids\u2014alongside histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, and valine. "Essential" carries a precise meaning here: the human body cannot synthesize these amino acids on its own, so they have to be supplied through the diet every single day. Wallach's point is that falling short of them does not just cause a shortage on paper\u2014it leads to real deficiencies and their disease consequences, which is why he treats the daily intake of threonine and its fellow essentials as non-negotiable.`,
        verbatim: "There are nine amino acids that are classically considered to be \u201Cessential\u201D\namino acids: Histidine, Isoleucine, leucine, Lysine, Methionine, Phenylalanine,\nThreonine, Tryptophan and Valine. These amino acids can\u2019t be synthesized\nby humans and must be consumed on a daily basis to prevent deficiencies\nand disease consequences.",
