        question: "What does the body make from omega-6 fatty acids?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says omega-6 EFAs are the raw material for lipoxins and other eicosanoid signaling compounds the body produces.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that the essential fatty acids are required to produce and maintain a whole family of signaling compounds: eicosanoids, endocannabinoids (which affect mood, behavior and inflammation), lipoxins and resolvins. He specifies that lipoxins -- a class of eicosanoid derivatives made through the lipoxygenase pathway -- come from omega-6 EFAs, while resolvins come from omega-3. So omega-6 is not just a structural fat; it is a starting material the body converts into inflammation- and signaling-regulating molecules.",
        verbatim: "The fatty acids are required for the production and maintenance of\neicosanoids, endocannabinoids (affecting mood, behavior, and inflammation),\nlipoxins (a class of eicosanoid derivatives through the lipoxygenase pathway\nfrom omega-6 EFAs), and resolvins from omega-3 (in the presence of\naspirin, downregulating inflammation)",
