        answer: "In Wallach's framework beryllium is one of the essential minerals, and his books describe it as a relatively rare element in both the universe and the earth's crust. Chemically it is a divalent metal that never occurs free in nature, showing up only bound into alloys or minerals, and it heads the 'alkaline-earth' family alongside calcium and magnesium. In pure form it is a hard, gray-white metal - one of the lightest metals, yet hard enough to scratch glass - and so reactive that it readily combines with oxygen and other elements. You mostly encounter it inside gemstones such as beryl, emerald, aquamarine and chrysoberyl.",
        verbatim: "Beryllium is a relatively rare element in the universe and the crust of the earth.\nIt is a divalent element that occurs naturally only in alloys with other elements\nor minerals. Gemstones that contain beryllium include beryl (aquamarine and\nemeralds) and chrysoberyl.",
