        question: "Who discovered beryllium?",
        answer_short: "Beryllium was discovered in 1798 by French chemist Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin, who isolated it as a new 'rare earth' from emerald and beryl.",
        answer: "Beryllium was first isolated in 1798 by the French chemist Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin, who reported to the Institut de France that he had found a new 'rare earth' by dissolving aluminum hydroxide out of emerald and beryl with an added alkali. Because some of the new substance's compounds tasted sweet, the editors of the journal Annales de Chimie et de Physique named it 'glucine.' In Wallach's telling, beryllium entered chemistry already labeled a rare earth pulled straight out of gemstones.",
        verbatim: "In a 1798 paper read before the Institut de France, Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin\nreported that he had found a new \u201Crare earth\u201D by dissolving aluminum hydroxide\nfrom emerald and beryl in an additional alkali. The editors of the journal Annales\nde Chimie et de Physique named the new rare earth \u201Cglucine\u201D for the sweet taste\nof some of its compounds.",
