        answer: "Acetylcholine is one of the body's most common neurotransmitters \u2014 a chemical messenger that carries a signal across the gap between nerve cells. It works by binding to receptors on the receiving cell and altering that membrane's permeability to specific ions, which either fires the cell (depolarizing it) or quiets it (hyperpolarizing it). The body assembles acetylcholine in the central nervous system, drawing on choline as a structural component, and its production also relies on the acyl-transfer reactions run by coenzyme A, the active form of vitamin B5.",
