        question: "Can arsenic be used to treat cancer?",
        answer_short: "Once a notorious poison, arsenic trioxide became an FDA-approved cancer drug in 2000; low doses put acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) patients into lasting remission.",
        answer: `Arsenic trioxide \u2014 the arsenic compound that 18th-century physician Thomas Fowler turned into a medicine \u2014 has been used medicinally for roughly 500 years, most often against cancer. In 2000 the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved it to treat acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), an often-fatal cancer of the blood and bone marrow. A 1998 New England Journal of Medicine report from Memorial Sloan-Kettering researchers found that low doses of arsenic trioxide destroyed the cancerous cells in 12 seriously ill APL patients and brought them into remission; Chinese researchers had reported the same result in 1997 (journal Blood), and their survivors were still leukemia-free ten years later. Arsenic is also used, as "Fowler's solution," to treat psoriasis.`,
