        answer_short: "Pellagra turned out to be a dietary deficiency disease \u2014 a lack of vitamin B3 (niacin) \u2014 and eggs were part of the fix. Wallach recounts how a U.S. Public Health Service researcher noticed that in orphanages and mental asylums the residents came down with pellagra while the staff, who ate a wider variety of food, stayed well. When eggs and milk were simply added to the sufferers' diets, the pellagra disappeared. Wallach lays out the competing theories of the era \u2014 the main one blamed the limited corn diet, while some held that pellagra was an insect-borne disease like malaria or yellow fever \u2014 and this is his illustration that nutrient-dense foods like eggs can reverse a 'disease' that was really malnutrition.",
        answer: "Pellagra turned out to be a dietary deficiency disease \u2014 a lack of vitamin B3 (niacin) \u2014 and eggs were part of the fix. Wallach recounts how a U.S. Public Health Service researcher noticed that in orphanages and mental asylums the residents came down with pellagra while the staff, who ate a wider variety of food, stayed well. When eggs and milk were simply added to the sufferers' diets, the pellagra disappeared. Wallach lays out the competing theories of the era \u2014 the main one blamed the limited corn diet, while some held that pellagra was an insect-borne disease like malaria or yellow fever, and sufferers were isolated in federal centers as though they were contagious like tuberculosis \u2014 and this is his illustration that nutrient-dense foods like eggs can reverse a 'disease' that was really malnutrition.",
