        question: "What is pernicious anemia and how is it related to B12?",
        answer_short: "Pernicious anemia is a fatal anemia, first reported in 1849, caused by missing vitamin B12; absorbing B12 requires both stomach acid and an acid-dependent 'intrinsic factor.'",
        answer: "In 1849 Thomas Addison described a fatal anemia, later named pernicious anemia, marked by a low count of abnormally enlarged red blood cells. Vitamin B12 was eventually identified as the missing nutrient, but the disease had a second twist: the stomach must produce two things, stomach acid and an acid-dependent 'intrinsic factor,' or B12 cannot be absorbed. Wallach notes that B12 injections can cure the disease by bypassing the stomach entirely, and that stomach-acid substitutes like betaine HCl can be used to reactivate intrinsic factor and restore absorption.",
        verbatim: "In 1849, Thomas Addison reported the existence of a hitherto \nunknown fatal anemia, that was eventually called pernicious anemia \n- it was characterized by a low red blood cell count, but the cells that \nwere observed were enlarged red blood cells. Vitamin B12 was even- \ntually identified as the missing essential nutrient, however, there was \nanother feature unique to pernicious anemia - the stomach had to \nproduce two important substances: 1) stomach acid and 2) an acid \ndependent cofactor known as \u201Cintrinsic factor\u201D - without either or \nboth vitamin B12 could not be absorbed.",
