        answer_short: "Yes \u2014 Wallach cites research linking chronic calcium deficiency to hypertension, with more than 30 studies supporting it and lower ionized calcium found in untreated hypertensives.",
        answer: 'Wallach ties chronic calcium deficiency to high blood pressure. In 1980 McCarron and colleagues theorized that chronic calcium deficiency leads to hypertension, and more than 30 later studies supported it; serum ionized calcium runs consistently lower in people with untreated hypertension. The link is clearest in people taking in less than 500 mg of calcium a day. In salt-sensitive hypertension, a rise in blood pressure tracks with urinary calcium loss from excess salt on top of an existing calcium deficiency \u2014 and among Black and elderly white people with essential hypertension, low calcium and potassium (rather than high salt) drive the "salt sensitivity." In a four-year study of 58,218 nurses, hypertension was more likely in women taking in less than 800 mg of calcium per day.',
        verbatim: "In 1980, McCarron et al theorized that chronic calcium\ndeficiency probably led to hypertension. More than 30\nsubsequent studies supported the original theory of calcium\ndeficiency as the cause of hypertension. In addition, recent\nstudies have shown that serum ionized calcium is consistently\nlower in humans with untreated hypertension.",
