        id: "WAL-CLM-IMMORT-000090",
        subject: "chromium",
        also_about: [],
        facet: "stance",
        question: "Can chromium help you live longer?",
        answer_short: "Wallach cites Gary Evans's finding that chromium supplementation increased the lifespan of laboratory animals by 33.3 percent, challenging the prior view that only severe calorie restriction could extend life.",
        answer: "Gary Evans of Bemidji State University (Minnesota) showed that simply supplementing chromium increased the lifespan of laboratory animals by 33.3% \u2014 a striking result, because until then the gerontologist Dr. Roy Walford and colleagues believed calorie restriction was the only proven way to extend life past the average. Wallach adds that chromium deficiency in humans brings a wide range of clinical diseases plus a shortened life expectancy, and that these are made worse by a simultaneous vanadium deficiency and by high sugar or alcohol intake.",
        verbatim: "Gary Evans, Bemidji State University, Minnesota, very\nclearly showed an increased lifespan in laboratory animals\nby 33.3%, when they were supplemented, with chromium.\nPrior to this study, gerontologist, led by Dr. Roy Walford,\nfelt a Calorie Restricted diet was the only way to extend life\npast the expected average age. Deficiencies of chromium in\nhumans are characterized by a wide variety of clinical diseases\nas well as a shortened life expectancy. The clinical diseases of\nchromium deficiency are aggravated by concurrent vanadium\ndeficiencies and increased sugar or alcohol intakes.",
        page: 132,
