        facet: "discovery",
        question: "How was copper deficiency first shown to cause neurological disease?",
        answer_short: "In 1937, enzootic ataxia (sway back) in newborn lambs was recognized as a copper deficiency in pregnant sheep \u2014 copper supplements prevented it \u2014 an early proof that copper deficiency causes neurological disease marked by demyelination of the cerebellum and spinal cord.",
        answer: "One of the earliest proofs that copper deficiency causes neurological disease. In 1937, 'enzootic ataxia' in newborn lambs \u2014 also called sway back or lamkruis, a loss of muscle coordination \u2014 was recognized as a copper deficiency in pregnant sheep. Giving the ewes copper supplements prevented it. The disease was marked by demyelination (loss of the protective myelin sheath around nerves) in the cerebellum (the brain's coordination center) and the spinal cord.",
        verbatim: "Neonatal \u201Cenzootic ataxia\u201D (sway back, lamkruis) was\nrecognized as a clinical and disease entity in 1937 as a copper\ndeficiency in pregnant sheep. Copper supplements prevented\nthe syndrome, which was characterized by demyelination of\nthe cerebellum and spinal cord.",
