        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does copper keep arteries and veins strong?",
        answer_short: "Copper runs the enzyme lysyl oxidase that turns proelastin into elastin, so a deficiency weakens vessel walls and produces spider veins, varicose veins, hemorrhoids and aneurysms.",
        answer: "The mechanism behind copper's cardiovascular effects. Copper activates numerous 'cupro-enzymes' (copper-dependent enzymes) that build and maintain the heart and blood vessels. In a copper-deficient pregnancy this causes congenital defects of the heart and vessels (Kawasaki disease) and of the brain (cerebral palsy, plus atresia \u2014 failure to form \u2014 and hypoplasia, or underdevelopment, of the cerebellum). Copper deficiency also lowers the activity of lysyl oxidase, the enzyme that converts proelastin into elastin; with less elastin, the walls of veins and arteries lose tensile strength \u2014 producing spider veins, varicose veins, hemorrhoids and aneurysms.",
        verbatim: "Copper functions as a cofactor and activator of numerous\ncupro-enzymes that are involved with the development and\nmaintenance of the cardiovascular system. Deficiency of\ncopper in the pregnant female results in congenital defects of\nthe heart and vascular system (Kawasaki disease) and brain\n(cerebral palsy, atresia and hypoplasia of the cerebellum).\nDeficiency of copper also results in reduced lysyl oxidase\nactivity causing a reduction in conversion of proelastin to\nelastin causing a decrease in the tensile strength of venous\nand arterial walls (spider veins, varicose veins, hemorrhoids\nand aneurysms).",
