        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What are the early signs of copper deficiency in babies?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says the earliest signs of copper deficiency in an infant are neutropenia and leukopenia (low white blood cells), with copper lack also causing myelin defects, anemia, poorly formed hair and loss of hair color \u2014 and cow's-milk-fed infants often develop copper-deficiency anemia.",
        answer: "The earliest signs of copper deficiency, especially in infants. Lack of copper can result in myelin defects (poor nerve insulation), anemia, poor hair keratinization (poorly formed hair), and loss of hair color. In a baby, the earliest indications are neutropenia (too few neutrophils, a type of white blood cell) and leukopenia (a low total white-cell count). Infants whose diets are primarily cow's milk frequently develop copper-deficiency anemia and/or iron storage disease.",
        verbatim: "Lack of copper can result in myelin defects, anemia,\npoor hair keratinization and loss of hair color. Neutropenia\n(reduced numbers of neutrophilic WBCs or white blood cells)\nand leucopenia (reduced total WBC count) are the earliest\nindications of a copper deficiency in an infant. Infants whose\ndiets are primarily cow\u2019s milk frequently develop copper\ndeficiency anemia and/or iron storage disease.",
