        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does fluoride protect teeth?",
        answer_short: "Fluoride lets the enamel-rebuilding process form fluorapatite, a version of enamel far more resistant to the acid that causes decay \u2014 which is why it's added to toothpaste.",
        answer: "How fluoride protects teeth \u2014 the mechanism behind fluoride toothpaste. Tooth enamel is made of a hard mineral, hydroxyapatite. Acids that mouth bacteria produce from sugary food and drink slowly dissolve it, and that loss of enamel starts tooth decay ('caries'). The body constantly fights back by rebuilding enamel (remineralization). When sodium fluoride is present, the rebuilt enamel forms as fluorapatite \u2014 a version considerably more resistant to acid damage than natural enamel. That acid-resistance is exactly why manufacturers add sodium fluoride to toothpaste.",
        verbatim: "The resulting loss of the hydroxyapatite enamel\n\n\npromotes the initiation of tooth decay or \u201Ccaries.\u201D One of the\nbody\u2019s defenses against the breakdown of hydroxyapatite is\nto generate new enamel continuously (remineralization).\n\nIn the presence of sodium fluoride, the remineralization\nprocess manufactures a form of enamel referred to as\nfluorapatite. Fluorapatite is considerably more resistant to\ndamage from acid than the natural enamel.",
        page: 141,
        book_id: "immortality",
