        facet: "warning",
        question: "Does fluoride cause cancer?",
        answer_short: "Wallach cites 1990 National Toxicology Program animal results in which fluoride raised precancerous oral-mucosa lesions, oral squamous-cell carcinoma, a rare osteosarcoma at double the rate in males, and thyroid and liver cancers, after Yiamouyiannis and Burk's 1977 charge of 10,000 excess cancer deaths from fluoridation.",
        answer: "The fluoride-cancer studies Wallach cites. Epidemiological work by Yiamouyiannis and Burk in 1977 led to full-scale U.S. congressional hearings over the charge that fluoridation of public water systems had caused 10,000 excess cancer deaths. The hearings ordered the U.S. National Toxicology Program to run animal studies focused on oral, liver and skeletal cancers. In 1990 those results showed, in rats, an increase in precancerous lesions of mucous-membrane cells; an increase in cancers of the oral mucous membranes (squamous cell carcinoma); a rare osteosarcoma (bone cancer) appearing at double the rate in males as in females; and increases in thyroid follicular-cell tumors and in liver cancer (hepatocholangiocarcinoma).",
        verbatim: "In 1990, the results of the fluoride study showed an\nincrease in rat precancerous lesions in mucus membrane cells.\nThere was an increase in cancers of the oral mucus membranes\n(squamous cell carcinoma). A rare form of osteosarcoma\nappeared at double the rate in males as females, and there was\nan increase in thyroid follicular cell tumors and liver cancer\n(hepatocholangiocarcinoma).",
        page: 142,
