        question: "Why does the body need iron?",
        answer_short: "Iron sits at the center of hemoglobin, where its atoms bind oxygen in the lungs and carry it to every cell.",
        answer: "Why iron is essential \u2014 it is what lets your blood carry oxygen. Iron is an essential nutrient for all land animals. Each hemoglobin molecule (the oxygen-carrying protein of red blood cells) is built around four iron atoms; oxygen chemically bonds to those iron atoms in the tiny blood vessels of the lungs (capillaries) and is then carried through the circulation to cells throughout the body. Iron was first recognized as an essential animal nutrient by Boussingault in the 1860s.",
        verbatim: "Iron is an essential nutrient for all terrestrial species\nof animals. It is the four iron atoms in each hemoglobin\nmolecule that are central to its structure and physiological\nfunction. Oxygen actually bonds to the iron atoms in the lung\ncapillaries, and then are transported via the circulation to\ndistant cells.",
