        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What is a normal blood pH?",
        answer_short: "Healthy blood runs 7.36 to 7.44. Below 7.30 is acidosis, above 7.44 is alkalosis, and below 6.8 or above 7.8 is rapidly fatal; inside cells pH runs 6.0 to 7.4.",
        answer: "The blood-pH thresholds for acidosis and alkalosis. When blood pH falls below 7.30 the person is in acidosis (too acidic); when it rises above 7.44 they are in alkalosis (too alkaline); a blood pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is rapidly fatal. Inside cells, pH runs 6.0 to 7.4. Rapid metabolism (as in an overactive thyroid, Graves' disease) or reduced blood flow (as in a heart attack) raises carbon-dioxide levels and so lowers pH \u2014 acidifying the blood.",
        verbatim: "When the pH falls below 7.30, the patient has acidosis; when\nthe blood pH rises above 7.44, the individual has alkalosis.\n\nBlood pH below 6.8 and above 7.8 is rapidly fatal.\nIntracellular pH ranges between 6.0 and 7.4. Rapid metabolism\n(hyperthyroid, Graves' disease) or decreased blood flow\n(heart attack) increases the carbon dioxide levels and therefore\ndecreases pH or acidifies the blood.",
