        facet: "stance",
        question: "Is margarine bad for you?",
        answer_short: "The hydrogenated and partially-hydrogenated vegetable oils sold as a safe substitute for butter \u2014 to prevent hardened arteries, fatty plaque and stroke \u2014 are now known to be a direct cause of those very diseases.",
        answer: "Hydrogenation and trans fats \u2014 Wallach's warning. Commercial food-makers pump hydrogen gas into liquid vegetable oils ('hydrogenation') to harden them into margarine, creating trans fatty acids. Wallach's stance: hydrogenated and partially-hydrogenated oils, once sold by the medical trade as a safe butter substitute to PREVENT hardening of the arteries (arteriosclerosis), fatty-plaque buildup (atherosclerosis) and stroke, are now known to be a DIRECT CAUSE of those very diseases. Fried foods, super-heated carbohydrates (breads, French fries, croutons), margarines, cooking oils and trans fats produce the inflammation that damages the arterial lining (intima), leading to clogged and blocked arteries, blood clots, thrombosis and stroke.",
        verbatim: "Hydrogenated and partially hydrogenated vegetable\noils, once touted by the medical trade as a safe substitute for\nbutter in the prevention of arteriosclerosis, atherosclerosis and\nstroke, are now known to be the direct cause of these diseases.\nThe consumption of fried foods, super-heated carbohydrates\n(breads, French fries, croutons, etc.), margarines, cooking\noils and trans fatty acids are now known to produce the\n\u201Cinflammation\u201D that damages the intima or arterial lining\nthat produces clogged and blocked arteries, blood clots,\nthrombosis and stroke.",
