        facet: "sources",
        question: "Do any famously long-lived people eat pork?",
        answer_short: "Yes \u2014 and they may be the best example there is. Wallach points to the Okinawans, whom the World Health Organization ranked in 1996 as the longest-lived people in the entire industrialized world, and boiled pork is a staple of their 1,500-calorie daily diet. He puts their life expectancy at 78 years for men and 86 for women, with a documented centenarian rate of 5 per 10,000 people \u2014 and, citing the 25-year Okinawa Centenarian Study, he credits their lifestyle and mineral-rich diet, not their genes, for that longevity. Their table alongside the pork includes chicken, eggs, dried minnows, miso and fish soup, tofu, sea and garden vegetables, kelp, garlic, purple sweet potatoes and bitter melon.",
        answer: "Yes \u2014 and they may be the best example there is. Wallach points to the Okinawans, whom the World Health Organization ranked in 1996 as the longest-lived people in the entire industrialized world, and boiled pork is a staple of their 1,500-calorie daily diet. He puts their life expectancy at 78 years for men and 86 for women, with a documented centenarian rate of 5 per 10,000 people \u2014 and, citing the 25-year Okinawa Centenarian Study, he credits their lifestyle and mineral-rich diet, not their genes, for that longevity. Their table alongside the pork includes chicken, eggs, dried minnows, miso and fish soup, tofu, sea and garden vegetables, kelp, garlic, purple sweet potatoes and bitter melon.",
        verbatim: "The Okinawan diet contains an average of 1,500 calories and\nconsists of boiled pork, chicken, eggs, dried minnows, miso\nand fish soup, tofu, many types of garden and sea vegetables,\nsea weed, kelp, onions, garlic, daikon, greens, purple sweet\npotatoes, bitter melon and tubers grown in their own gardens;",
        page: null,
