        answer_short: "Wallach says yes - how many you have and how dark they are tracks your free-radical damage, and forecasts cataracts, heart disease, diabetes and dementia.",
        answer: "Wallach treats age spots as a readable gauge of internal damage: how many you have and how dark they are tracks your level of free-radical damage directly. He calls them a predictive barometer for cataracts, macular degeneration, coronary artery disease, diabetes, senile dementia, Alzheimer's disease and cancer. The reasoning follows from what the pigment is - the same ceroid lipofuscin is accumulating in the brain, heart, liver, lungs, bone marrow and eyes, where it cannot be seen.",
        verbatim: "The number and intensity of pigment levels in age\nspots are directly related to the level of free radical damage\nand can be a predictive barometer of sinister things to come -\ni.e. - cataracts, macular degeneration, coronary artery disease,\ndiabetes, senile dementia, Alzheimer\u2019s disease, cancer, etc.",
