        facet: "sources",
        question: "What do the world's longest-lived people actually eat?",
        answer_short: "Dark fruits and berries, yellow and red vegetables, cocoa, red wine, green tea and coffee - every day, not occasionally.",
        answer: "The world's longest-lived people eat dark fruits and berries, yellow and red vegetables, cocoa, red wine, green tea and coffee - every day rather than occasionally. Wallach maps compound to source: lycopenes from tomatoes, leutines from green leafy vegetables, proanthocyanins from red grape skins and seeds, resveratrol from red wine, red grape juice and giant Japanese knotweed, and flavonols and polyphenols from dark chocolate, red wine and green tea. What he stresses is the constancy - seasonal variety changes with the growing season, but daily consumption of multiple antioxidant sources does not.",
        verbatim: 'In the "Blue Zones" of the world, large quantities of vitamin C, Beta carotene,\nflavanols, lycopenes, leutenes, proanthocyanins, resveratrols, catechens (EGCGs) and\npolyphenols are found in dark fruits and berries, yellow and red vegetables, cocoa, red\nwine, green tea or coffee - these are the antioxidants that have universally been found\nto be consumed daily in the Blue Zones and are known to protect the centenarians.',
        page: null,
