        answer: "Wallach's measure of the gap is stark: the industrialized world should be averaging 95 to 100 years, and instead averages about 75 - some 20 to 25 years short of our genetic potential. He tracks the American slide with CDC and WHO rankings: 17th in the world for longevity in 1990, down to 24th by 2000, down to a pitiful 46th by 2005, and 48th by 2008.",
        verbatim: "the\naverage age of peoples in the industrialized world should\nbe somewhere between 95 and 100 years of age. However,\nthe average age of those living in the industrialized world\nis somewhere around 75 years of age (give or take five\nyears), in fact 20 to 25 years short of our genetic potential for\nlongevity.",
