        answer_short: "1,250 to 1,800 a day - but only WITHOUT malnutrition, meaning every essential nutrient at optimal levels inside those calories.",
        answer: "The second longevity piece is a calorie-restricted diet WITHOUT malnutrition - a distinction Wallach says most practitioners of calorie restriction miss entirely. It is universally known to extend the life span of laboratory, pet and farm animals, and it is a common thread among centenarians both historically and in every Blue Zone today. The levels he cites are 1,250 to 1,800 calories a day, with those doing heavier physical labour at the upper end. The 'without malnutrition' half means every known essential nutrient at optimal levels, well above the RDI, in fewer calories - which is why the mineral piece and the calorie piece are inseparable.",
        verbatim: "In most cases the caloric levels fall between 1,250 and\n1,800 calories per day, with those performing more physical\nlabor consuming the higher calorie levels.",
