        answer: "EDTA has a striking origin: calcium EDTA was a man-made amino acid the Germans engineered just before World War I to antidote the arsenic and lead of chemical-warfare exposure \u2014 it was born as a heavy-metal antidote. That same claw-like binding ('chelate' is Latin for claw) is why it is used today for intravenous chelation therapy to open obstructed arteries, and Wallach folds IV EDTA chelation into his programs for cardiovascular problems \u2014 poor circulation, arteriosclerosis, stroke. Do not confuse it with the 'chelated minerals' on a supplement label (a livestock-industry invention for better absorption) or with plant-derived colloidal minerals \u2014 EDTA is the therapeutic chelator that grabs metals and pulls them out.",
        verbatim: "The original chelating agent use was calcium EDTA, a\nman-made amino acid that was created by the Germans just\nprior to WWI to antidote arsenic and lead exposure in chemical\nwarfare attacks (calcium EDTA is used today for intravenous\nchelation therapy to open up obstructed arteries).",
