        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is magnesium?",
        answer_short: "Magnesium is a mineral no living thing can do without. In Wallach's framework it switches on a vast number of the body's enzymes, carries out electro-chemical, catalytic and structural jobs, and is the metal atom at the center of the chlorophyll in every green plant.",
        answer: "In Wallach's account magnesium is a white, silvery, very lightweight metal that is essential to all living organisms. It works as a cofactor with electro-chemical, catalytic and structural roles, activating numerous enzymes throughout the body, and it is a constituent of every chlorophyll molecule -- which is why Wallach calls it the ultimate source of all biological energy. An adult body holds 20 to 28 grams of magnesium, most of it locked in bone and skeletal muscle. Wallach treats magnesium as the calcium 'relaxer' and blames a shortage for problems ranging from muscle cramps, migraines and heart palpitations to the arterial 'malignant calcification' he pins on magnesium deficiency rather than on cholesterol.",
        verbatim: "Magnesium, as a mineral is essential to all living\norganisms and has cofactor, electro-chemical, catalytic, and\nstructural functions. It activates numerous enzymes and is a\nconstituent of all chlorophylls.",
        page: null,
