        question: "How much magnesium is in the human body?",
        answer_short: "Wallach puts total body magnesium at 20 to 28 grams in an adult, with about 60% locked in the bones and roughly a quarter in skeletal muscle.",
        answer: "Wallach states that an adult human carries 20 to 28 grams of magnesium in total, with roughly 60% stored in bone and about 26% in skeletal muscle; the rest is spread across organs and body fluids. In the blood, magnesium runs 1.5 to 2.1 mEq/L and is second only to potassium as an intracellular cation. About half of the body's magnesium \u2014 most of it bound in bone \u2014 is not exchangeable, meaning it can't be readily called on when you run short.",
        verbatim: "The adult human contains 20 to 28 grams of total body\nMg. Approximately 60% is found in bone. Twenty-six percent\nis associated with skeletal muscle and the balance is distributed\nbetween various organs and body fluids. Serum levels of Mg\nrange from 1.5 to 2.1 mEq/L (a measure of electrolytes) and is\nsecond to potassium as an intracellular cation. Half of the Mg,\nincluding most that is bound in bone, is not exchangeable.",
