        facet: "sources",
        question: "How does the body make vitamin D from sunlight?",
        answer_short: "In the skin, sunlight or UV light drives the conversion of cholesterol into 7-dehydrocholesterol, the provitamin of vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol); UV light also converts the yeast sterol ergosterol into vitamin D2.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that cholesterol is converted by the intestinal mucosa into 7-dehydrocholesterol, the provitamin of vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol), and that the skin and other tissues also carry out this process when exposed to ultraviolet light and sunlight. Separately, ergosterol, a yeast sterol, is converted into vitamin D2 on exposure to sunlight or UV light. This is why sun exposure is a primary source of vitamin D and why cholesterol is its raw material.",
        verbatim: "Ergosterol, a yeast\nsterol, is converted to vitamin D2 when exposed to ultraviolet\nlight. Cholesterol is converted by the intestinal mucosa\nto 7-dehydrocholesterol, the provitamin of vitamin D3,\ncholecalciferol. This process is also carried out by the skin and\nother tissues, when exposed to ultraviolet light and sunlight.",
