        facet: "uses",
        question: "Can vitamin D prevent cancer and chronic disease?",
        answer_short: "Wallach cites research that vitamin D deficiency raises the risk of heart attacks, breast and colon cancer mortality, and childhood type II diabetes, while adequate vitamin D may help prevent many serious chronic diseases.",
        answer: "Wallach cites Dr. Michael Holick of Boston University, who said the bottom line is that vitamin D is important for the health of both children and adults and may help prevent many serious chronic diseases. He reports Holick's finding that vitamin D deficiency in adults increases the risk of heart attacks, increases mortality in breast and colon cancer victims, raises mortality in dialysis patients, and increases the risk of type II diabetes in children. This positions vitamin D as protective against major chronic diseases, not just rickets.",
        verbatim: "In 2006 Dr. Michael Holick, a professor of medicine,\nphysiology and biophysics at Boston University said, \u201CThe\nbottom line is, we now recognize that vitamin D, is important\nfor health for both children and adults and may help prevent\nmany serious chronic diseases.\u201D\nHolick\u2019s studies have shown that deficiencies of\nvitamin D, in adults results in an increase in risk of heart\n\nattacks, increased mortality in breast and colon cancer\n\n\n \n\nvictims, increased risk of mortality in dialysis patients and an\nincreased risk of developing type II diabetes in children.",
