        answer: "Wallach explains that fats spare thiamine because they can be metabolized for energy without the need for it, whereas the metabolism of carbohydrate requires thiamine. In practical terms, a diet high in sugar and refined carbohydrate increases the body's demand for vitamin B1. Fats also aid in the transport and absorption of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K.",
        verbatim: "Fats aid in the transport and absorption of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K. Fats spare thiamin since they\ncan be metabolized for energy without the need for thiamin,\nwhereas, the metabolism of carbohydrate requires thiamine.",
