        facet: "discovery",
        question: "How was vitamin B2 (riboflavin) discovered?",
        answer_short: "Riboflavin was first seen in 1879 as a yellow-green pigment in milk whey, isolated in 1932, then synthesized and named in 1935 by Kuhn.",
        answer: "Wallach recounts that riboflavin (vitamin B2) was recognized in 1879 as a yellow-green fluorescent pigment in milk whey, but its biological significance was not understood until 1932, when a team of German investigators isolated 'Warburg's yellow enzyme' from yeast and demonstrated that the substance was necessary for an intracellular enzyme to function. The substance was synthesized in 1935 by Kuhn, who gave it the name riboflavin.",
        verbatim: "Riboflavin or vitamin B, was recognized in 1879 as a\nyellow-green fluorescent pigment in milk whey. The biological\nsignificance of this yellow pigment was not understood\nuntil 1932 when a team of German investigators isolated\n\u201CWarburg\u2019s yellow enzyme\u201D from yeast and demonstrated\nthat the substance was necessary for the functioning of an\nintracellular enzyme. The substance was synthesized in 1935\nby Kuhn and named riboflavin.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "immortality",
        topics: [
          "history",
          "discovery",
          "when was it discovered",
          "who discovered it"
        ]
