        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is Vitamin B5?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin B5, or pantothenic acid, is a water-soluble B-complex vitamin and the core building block of coenzyme A - the molecule the body uses to turn carbohydrates, fats, and proteins into energy.",
        answer: "Vitamin B5, also called pantothenic acid, is one of the essential B-complex vitamins. In Wallach's framework it is indispensable because it forms part of coenzyme A, the molecule that runs two-carbon unit metabolism. Through coenzyme A the body requires B5 to metabolize all three major food groups - carbohydrates, lipids (fats), and proteins - which places it at the center of energy production and the manufacture of fatty acids, steroid hormones, and the neurotransmitter acetylcholine. Because it is water-soluble and continuously used up, it must be supplied through diet and daily supplementation; a shortfall shows up as burning feet, dermatitis, fatigue, depression, and a racing heartbeat.",
        verbatim: "Pantothenic acid or vitamin B. is part of coenzyme A,\nwhich functions in two-carbon unit metabolism. Pantothenic\nacid is required for the metabolism of carbohydrates, lipids\nand proteins.",
