        question: "What is Vitamin B12?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a red, water-soluble vitamin whose color and biological activity both come from a single cobalt atom at the center of its molecule.",
        answer: "In Wallach's framework, vitamin B12 -- also called cobalamin -- is a red, water-soluble vitamin, and both its color and its function trace to a single cobalt atom held at the center of the molecule, which makes B12 the body's main way of using the essential mineral cobalt. It is a fragile compound, slowly deactivated by acid, alkali, light, and oxidizing or reducing substances, and about 30% of its activity is destroyed during cooking. B12 works together with folic acid to build DNA and RNA, supports the bone marrow in making red blood cells, and is needed to form the myelin that insulates nerves -- so a deficiency shows up as pernicious anemia and progressive nerve damage. Because only bacteria, fungi, and algae can actually manufacture it, B12 reaches people mainly through animal foods and supplements, and absorbing it depends on adequate stomach acid plus an acid-dependent 'intrinsic factor.'",
        verbatim: "Vitamin B12 is a red crystalline substance that is water\nsoluble. The red color is due to the cobalt atom in the molecule.",
