        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is copper?",
        answer_short: "Copper is an essential nutrient for every living organism and a cofactor for many hundreds of enzymes; Wallach considers copper deficiency widespread, common, and behind many everyday diseases.",
        answer: "Copper is a genuine essential nutrient -- every living organism needs it. Wallach frames it as a universally important cofactor, the helper mineral that many hundreds of the body's metalloenzymes (enzymes built around a metal atom) depend on to work. Through those enzymes copper builds the pigment in hair and skin, forms the elastic fibers that give tensile strength to blood vessels, skin and vertebral discs, and helps cells use oxygen. He stresses that copper deficiency is widespread and common, and that copper-deficiency diseases have a real, under-recognized impact on human populations -- which is why he ties so many conditions, from gray hair to aneurysms, back to too little copper.",
        verbatim: "Copper is an essential nutrient for all living organisms\nand is a universally important cofactor for many hundreds\nof metalloenzymes. Copper deficiency is widespread and\ncommon and copper deficiency diseases have an important\nimpact on human populations.",
