        answer_short: "Iodine is a violet-black halogen element the body needs to build thyroid hormone; run short of it and the thyroid swells into a goiter.",
        answer: "Iodine is a violet-black solid element, the heaviest and one of the most reactive members of the halogen family, found in seaweed, brine wells, and the sea. In Wallach's framework it is an essential nutrient for all vertebrates: the body combines it with the amino acid (protein building block) tyrosine to manufacture thyroxin, the main thyroid hormone that regulates metabolism, heart rate, and body temperature. When iodine runs short -- which Wallach says is common, especially since many Americans cut back on salt on their doctors' advice -- the thyroid gland enlarges into a goiter and the body slows into hypothyroidism.",
        verbatim: "Iodine is a violet-black solid that vaporizes to give a\nviolet gas. It is found in sea weed and brine wells, in the sea,\nin the form of inorganic salts and organic iodides. It is the\nheaviest element in the halogen family and is highly reactive.",
