        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does vitamin E slow aging?",
        answer_short: "Vitamin E protects the fatty membranes of your cells from peroxidation (free-radical damage), and that protection is tied to its anti-aging effect.",
        answer: "Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant that shields the lipids in cell membranes from peroxidation, a form of free-radical damage. Wallach explains that this ability to protect membrane fats from oxidation is directly related to vitamin E's anti-aging properties. In the same role it prevents oxidation damage to polyunsaturated fatty acids and protects cellular and mitochondrial membranes, helping keep cells from breaking down over time.",
        verbatim: "The ability of vitamin E to protect membrane lipids from\nperoxidation has been related to its anti-aging properties.",
        page: null,
