        answer_short: "Choline builds sphingomyelin in the brain and peripheral nerves and is the precursor for acetylcholine, so it is involved in transmitting nerve impulses.",
        answer: "Choline is a component of the phospholipid sphingomyelin, an important part of the brain and peripheral nerves. Because it is a precursor for the neurotransmitter acetylcholine, choline is directly involved in the transmission of nerve impulses.",
        verbatim: "Choline is also a component of another phospholipid,\nsphingomyelin, which is an important component of the\nbrain and peripheral nerves. As a precursor for acetylcholine,\ncholine is involved in the transmission of nerve impulses.",
