        answer: "Because choline is a raw material for acetylcholine \u2014 a key nerve-signaling chemical \u2014 and a building block of the brain's and nerves' insulating sphingomyelin, Wallach reports it has real value in movement disorders. Large doses of choline, he says, have proven beneficial for managing and easing the symptoms of tardive dyskinesia (the involuntary movements that can follow long-term drug use) and of Huntington's disease in people. He notes, too, that a choline shortage produces a 'fatty liver.'",
