        facet: "basics",
        question: "Is germanium used in electronics or computers?",
        answer_short: "Germanium is a poor conductor classed as a semiconductor, and when 'doped' with trace elements it is used to make transistors and microcomputers.",
        answer: "Germanium is a poor conductor of electricity, which places it in semiconductor status. Adding tiny amounts of other elements such as arsenic, gallium, or antimony -- a process called 'doping' -- greatly increases its ability to conduct electricity. Doped germanium is used to manufacture transistors, which essentially become micro-computers.",
        verbatim: "Germanium is a poor conductor of electricity\nand therefore is relegated to a semi-conductor status.\n\nThe addition of small amounts of other elements such\nas arsenic, gallium, or antimony is called \u201Cdoping\u201D - this\nprocess greatly increases its ability to conduct electricity.\nDoped germanium is used to manufacture transistors, which\nessentially become micro-computers.",
