        question: "What is boron?",
        answer_short: "Boron is a scarce trace mineral Wallach counts among the nutrients essential for human health, needed for bone metabolism, the efficient use of calcium and magnesium, and normal sex-hormone levels.",
        answer: "Boron is a scarce, non-metallic trace mineral that never occurs pure in nature, turning up instead in combined compounds such as borax and boric acid. Wallach counts it among the nutrients essential for human health. In his framework boron is required for bone metabolism and the efficient use of calcium and magnesium, for the proper function of the endocrine glands (the ovaries, testes, and adrenals), and for maintaining normal blood levels of estrogen and testosterone. Although it was accepted as a human essential only in 1990, it now sits firmly among the minerals Wallach says the body must have.",
        verbatim: "Boron is essential for bone metabolism including the\nefficient use of calcium and magnesium and proper function\nof endocrine glands (i.e. - ovaries, testes and adrenals).",
