        facet: "sources",
        question: "Why do older people often need B12 shots?",
        answer_short: "Cobalt/B12 only absorbs when intrinsic factor is present and stomach pH is 2.0 or less; low stomach acid (often from too little salt) blocks it, so doctors give shots.",
        answer: "Wallach explains that metallic cobalt is absorbed at only 20 to 26.2%, and only if intrinsic factor is present and the stomach pH is 2.0 or less. Intrinsic factor is a mucoprotein enzyme (Castle's intrinsic factor) that is part of normal stomach secretions. When a person has hypochlorhydria - low stomach acid, usually from a NaCl (salt) deficiency - the intrinsic factor won't work and B12/cobalt is not absorbed, which is why doctors frequently give B12 shots to older people on salt-restricted diets.",
        verbatim: "Metallic cobalt itself is absorbed at the rate of 20 to\n26.2% by mice and in humans if intrinsic factor is present\nin the stomach and the stomach pH is 2.0 or less. Intrinsic\nfactor is a mucoprotein enzyme known as Castle\u2019s intrinsic\nfactor and is part of normal stomach secretions. If a person\nhas hypochlorhydria (low stomach acid- usually a NaCl\ndeficiency) the intrinsic factor will not work and B12 cobalt is\nnot absorbed - this is why doctors frequently give B12 shots\nto older people on salt restricted diets.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "immortality",
