        question: "What is sodium?",
        answer_short: "Sodium is a soft, highly reactive silvery metal and the sixth most abundant element in the earth's crust, known best as sodium chloride \u2014 common table salt. Wallach treats it as an essential macro-mineral and one of the body's three indispensable electrolytes.",
        answer: "In Wallach's mineral encyclopedia, sodium is a highly reactive, bright, silvery metal \u2014 light enough to float on water and soft enough to cut with a knife \u2014 and the sixth most abundant element in the earth's crust. People know it best as sodium chloride, ordinary table salt. Wallach classes it as an essential macro-mineral and one of three indispensable electrolytes (with chloride and potassium) that maintain the body's water balance, osmotic and acid-base equilibrium, and nerve and muscle function. Far from being the villain conventional medicine makes it out to be, in his framework salt is vital to life \u2014 it is the raw material the stomach uses to make hydrochloric acid \u2014 and the real danger lies in restricting it, not in eating it.",
