        facet: "sources",
        question: "Is processed table salt as good as natural salt?",
        answer_short: "No \u2014 unprocessed salt once carried trace minerals and rare earths, while modern processed salt fools the body into thinking it is getting minerals.",
        answer: 'Wallach says that historically, eating salt to satisfy a pica craving had real value because unprocessed salt often contained trace minerals and rare earths. He argues salt consumption today is not, in and of itself, harmful \u2014 but processed salt creates the problem of letting the body think it is getting minerals, the equivalent of an "empty calorie" diet of processed-food calories without vitamins.',
        verbatim: "Historically, the consumption of salt to satisfy a pica\nbehavior was of value because salt was not processed and did\noften times contain trace minerals and rare earths.\n\nToday, salt consumption, although contrary to popular\nbelief, is notin and of itself harmful; it does present the problem\nof allowing our bodies to think we are getting minerals (the\nequivalent of the \u201Cempty calorie diets,\u201D i.e. - processed food\ncalories without vitamins).",
