        subject: "sodium",
        also_about: [],
        facet: "discovery",
        question: "Who discovered sodium and where does its name come from?",
        answer_short: "Sir Humphry Davy isolated sodium in 1807; the name comes from 'soda,' from the Latin 'sodanum,' an ancient Roman headache remedy.",
        answer: "Sodium was first isolated in 1807 by the English chemist Sir Humphry Davy, Wallach recounts, who separated the reactive, silvery metal from molten sodium hydroxide. Its name carries an old-medicine root: 'sodium' comes from 'soda,' which traces back to the Latin 'sodanum,' a common headache remedy in ancient Rome. He adds that the pure metal is light enough to float on water and soft enough to cut with a knife.",
        verbatim: "The English chemist, Sir Humphry Davy first isolated and\nidentified sodium as a new element in 1807 by separating it from\nmolten sodium hydroxide. The name, sodium is taken from the\nEnglish word soda, which itself is derived from the Latin word\nsodanum, a common headache remedy of ancient Rome.",
