        question: "What is silica?",
        answer_short: "Silica is silicon dioxide (SiO2), the most important compound of silicon \u2014 the second most abundant element in the earth's crust after oxygen \u2014 and Wallach counts it among the body's essential minerals.",
        answer: "In Wallach's framework, silica is silicon dioxide (SiO2), the most important compound of the element silicon. Silicon is the second most abundant element in the earth's crust, exceeded only by oxygen, and its oxygen-bonded compounds make up most of the world's sand, rock and soil. Silica occurs in two familiar natural forms \u2014 a crystalline form called quartz, the tiny chips of which are the grains in sand, and a noncrystalline form called flint \u2014 while gems such as amethyst, opal, agate and jasper are simply quartz crystals carrying colored impurities. Wallach treats silica as an essential mineral, one needed for the optimal health of the bones, skin, hair and nails.",
        verbatim: "The most important silicon compound is silica, or\nsilicon dioxide (SiO,). It exists in two familiar forms in nature:\na crystalline form called quartz, small chips of which are\nfound in sand, a a noncrystalline form called flint. Small gems\nand semiprecious stones such as amethyst, opal, agate and\njasper are crystals of quartz with colored impurities.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "immortality",
