        answer_short: "A painful, sometimes fatal lung disease caused by inhaling beryllium oxide dust from mining and industry; its chronic form resembles pulmonary sarcoidosis.",
        answer: "Berylliosis is a painful and sometimes fatal lung disease caused by inhaling beryllium oxide dust, which is released as a contaminant during the mining and industrial handling of the metal. Beryllium itself is a mineral \u2014 the scientific name of the gemstone beryl is beryllium aluminum silicate \u2014 so the hazard comes not from the element in food but from breathing its powdered oxide. Wallach describes two forms: an acute form (acute beryllium disease, or chemical pneumonitis) first reported in Europe in 1933 and in the United States in 1943, and a chronic form \u2014 chronic berylliosis \u2014 that resembles pulmonary sarcoidosis, another chronic lung disease.",
        verbatim: "Inhalation of beryllium oxide dust (mining and\nindustrial contaminants) can lead to \u201Cberylliosis,\u201D a painful\nand sometimes fatal lung disease.",
