        facet: "history",
        question: "Who kept chicken heart cells alive for 34 years?",
        answer_short: "Dr. Alexis Carrel grew fibroblast 'stem cells' from chicken hearts that kept multiplying for 34 years, giving rise to the idea of cellular immortality.",
        answer: "Wallach recounts that in 1912 Dr. Alexis Carrel, a Nobel laureate for pioneering end-to-end anastomosis (the suturing together of blood vessels), began growing undifferentiated fibroblasts, connective-tissue cells he calls 'stem cells,' from chicken hearts in flasks at the Rockefeller Institute. Fed on filtered extracts of blended chicken embryos, these cultures kept multiplying and growing for 34 years, including two years after Carrel's own death. Wallach presents this as the basis of 'cellular immortality': the theory that cells are inherently immortal if they are fed a perfect diet and kept in a perfect environment.",
        verbatim: "In 1912, Dr. Alexis Carrel, who had won the Nobel\nPrize for developing the original technique for \u201Cend to end\nanastomosis (joining) of blood vessels with fine suture,\u201D\nbegan to grow undifferentiated fibroblasts (connective tissue\ncells aka \u201Cstem cells\u201D) from chicken hearts in flasks in his\nlaboratory at the Rockefeller Institute.\n\nHe fed these isolated and undifferentiated fibroblast or\n\u201Cstem cells\u201D with filtered extracts of blended chicken embryos\nand the \u201Cstem cells\u201D kept multiplying and growing for 34\nyears (including 2 years after Carrel\u2019s own death).",
        page: null,
