        answer: `Wallach teaches that muscle cramps and twitches \u2014 including eyelid (eye) twitches \u2014 are signs of calcium deficiency even when the blood calcium reading stays within the "normal" range. He explains that calcium is depleted from the cells (severely enough to cause life-threatening convulsions) before blood calcium levels ever fall, so a normal blood calcium does not rule out deficiency. Calcium's reference range is 9\u201310.8 mg per 100 ml.`,
        verbatim: 'Muscle cramps and twitches\n(eye twitches) are signals of calcium deficiency (although the blood calcium will be in\nthe "normal" range). Serious life threatening\nconvulsions from low cell levels of calcium\nwill occur before low blood levels show up.\nThe normal range is 9-10.8 mg',
