        answer_short: "Homeopathic-style flower preparations from bacteriologist Edward Bach (1880-1936), who first collected sun-warmed flower dew; aimed at emotional imbalance rather than physical disease, taken as four drops four times a day.",
        answer: "Wallach describes Bach Flower Remedies, conceived by Edward Bach (1880-1936), who began his career as a bacteriologist at University College Hospital, London, and as pathologist and bacteriologist at the London Homeopathic Hospital. Bach initially collected sun-warmed dew from flowers, later expanding his 'remedies' to a homeopathic-like water preparation of flowers; traditionally the patient takes four drops of the diluted remedy four times per day. Wallach notes Bach's remedies were designed to address emotional imbalances rather than physical disease. (Scope note: Bach flower therapy is a homeopathy-class system using highly diluted preparations; included as Wallach's descriptive account of the modality.)",
        verbatim: "Bach Remedies (Bach Flower Remedies) were conceived by Edward Bach (1880-\n1936). Bach started his career as a bacteriologist in the University College Hospital in London and as the pathologist and bacteriologist\nto the London Homeopathic Hospital",
