        answer_short: "Wallach calls cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease) a WHO-recognized selenium-deficiency disease that about $1/month of selenium would prevent \u2014 the same as 'white muscle disease' in livestock \u2014 and treats early cardiomyopathy with selenium (oral or IM), plant-derived colloidal selenium, vitamin E, and essential fatty acids.",
        answer: "Cardiomyopathy (Keshan disease, or muscular dystrophy of the heart) is recognized by the World Health Organization as a selenium-deficiency disease, and is the kind of heart disease that makes a patient a transplant candidate in conventional medicine \u2014 yet Wallach notes about $1 per month of selenium would prevent it. It is the same disease veterinarians call 'mulberry heart' in pigs and 'white muscle disease' in livestock, eliminated in animals with selenium. Treatment of early cardiomyopathy includes selenium at 350 micrograms three times daily or 1,000 micrograms by intramuscular injection, plant-derived colloidal selenium, vitamin E at 1,200 international units per day, and essential fatty acids at 5 grams three times daily.",
        verbatim: "The treatment of early cardiomyopathy includes selenium at 350 mcg t.i.d. or 1,000 mcg\nIM, plant derived colloidal selenium, vitamin\nE at 1,200 IU/day and essential fatty acids at 5\ngm t.i.d",
