        answer_short: "Wallach usually manages a hiatal hernia by controlling gas and dyspepsia with small meals and sleeping with the head of the bed raised, reserving surgical repair for a large diaphragm defect to prevent strangulation.",
        answer: "Hiatal hernia is usually a congenital birth defect (not genetic) but can also be caused by crushing the chest or abdomen, as in a car accident; gas, dyspepsia, reflux, and chest pain that mimics a heart attack are common symptoms, and Wallach notes more than 40% of the USA population has asymptomatic hiatal hernia by x-ray survey. Treatment is usually restricted to managing the gas and dyspepsia: small meals, and sleeping with the head of the bed elevated to prevent nighttime esophageal reflux. When there is a large defect in the diaphragm (paraesophageal hiatal hernia), surgical repair is recommended to prevent strangulation of portions of the stomach, intestine, or liver.",
