        answer: "Hysteria (melancholia) is, in Wallach's framing, a symptomatic diagnosis that can result from premenstrual syndrome, food allergies, prescribed and illegal drugs, alcoholism, and hypoglycemia. Work-up: test for food allergies (pulse test), hypoglycemia (six-hour glucose tolerance test), and premenstrual syndrome (a daily diary), and review any medications. Treatment: avoid offending (allergenic) foods; rotation diets; autoimmune urine therapy; essential fatty acids at 5 grams three times daily with meals; chromium at 50-200 micrograms three times daily with meals; betaine hydrochloride and pancreatic enzymes at 75-200 milligrams three times daily before meals; acupuncture and homeopathy; herbs black hellebore (Helleborus niger), blue cohosh (Caulophyllum thalictroides), and mistletoe (Viscum album); vitamin B-3 at 450 milligrams three times daily (time-release); and vitamin B-6 at 150-300 milligrams three times daily (take for thirty days, then a 14-day break to avoid side effects).",
