        answer_short: "Keratomalacia is a hazy, dry, ulcerating cornea from vitamin A deficiency that can end in permanent blindness; Wallach treats it with beta-carotene vitamin A, zinc, more animal protein, and the baseline nutritional program.",
        answer: "Keratomalacia (xerophthalmia) is a hazy, dry cornea (the clear domed bulge at the front of the eye) that becomes ulcerated \u2014 a sign of vitamin A deficiency. Symptoms include extreme dryness of the eyes with blinking to keep them moist, conjunctivitis, night blindness, and fat-like spots (Bitot's spots) on the white of the eyeball; untreated, it can cause permanent blindness. Treatment: vitamin A at 25,000 international units (children) to 300,000 international units (adults) per day as beta carotene, zinc at 10-50 milligrams three times daily, and increasing animal protein to 120 grams per day (chicken, fish, eggs, milk, lamb, pork, or beef), plus the baseline nutritional program.",
