        answer: "Legionnaires' disease is caused by infection with Legionella pneumophila; it is characterized by pneumonia, high fever, slow heart rate, dry cough, chills, pleuritis, and diarrhea. A relatively new disease (discovered in 1976), it is fatal in 80% of untreated cases and is diagnosed by growing the organism on charcoal-yeast extract from sputum; immunosuppressed patients (those on chemotherapy, transplant patients, AIDS patients) are most susceptible. Treatment is specific: erythromycin at 500 milligrams to 1 gram orally or intravenously every 6 hours for three weeks.",
