        answer_short: "Wallach says leptospirosis (Weil's disease) responds best to antibiotics started within four days of onset, with tetracycline at 500 milligrams four times daily being effective.",
        answer: "Leptospirosis (Weil's disease, infectious jaundice) is an infectious disease caused by Leptospira, a spirochete bacterium carried by dogs, rats, and various wild animals; people are infected through urine-contaminated water or cuts while skinning or butchering infected animals. Symptoms include anemia, jaundice, proteinuria, hematuria, and occasionally aseptic meningitis, with fever and chills consistent; diagnosis needs blood tests or urine cultures. Treatment with antibiotics is most effective if started within four days of onset \u2014 tetracycline at 500 milligrams four times daily is effective.",
