        facet: "protocol",
        question: "How does Wallach treat leukemia?",
        answer_short: "Wallach's leukemia program includes laetrile, hydrogen peroxide, IV DMSO, Onco-Tox, shark liver extract, hydrazine sulfate, cesium chloride, polyerga, germanium, and carbamide, plus as-needed micro-dose chemotherapy, IV amino acids, vitamin C to bowel tolerance, selenium at 500-1,000 mcg/day, and vitamin A at 300,000 IU as beta carotene.",
        answer: "Leukemia (blood cancer) is a cancer of the blood-forming tissues of the bone marrow; no exact cause is proven, though viruses, radiation, and chemicals such as benzene are implicated. It produces a defect in white-blood-cell maturation, flooding the circulation with immature white cells; diagnosis comes from a blood test showing anemia, low platelets, increased lymphoblasts, and an elevated total white-cell count, and symptoms include weakness, joint pain, anemia, enlarged lymph nodes, and an enlarged spleen. Wallach's treatment includes Laetrile, hydrogen peroxide, intravenous dimethyl sulfoxide (said to mature the immature cells), Onco-Tox, shark liver extract, hydrazine sulfate, cesium chloride, polyerga, germanium, carbamide, and as needed micro-dose chemotherapy, intravenous amino acids, vitamin C to bowel tolerance, selenium at 500-1,000 micrograms per day, and vitamin A at 300,000 international units as beta carotene.",
        verbatim: "Treatment includes Laetrile, hydrogen peroxide, DMSO (matures the immature cells in the\ncirculation) IV, Onco-Tox, shark liver extract,\nhydrazine sulfate, cesium chloride, polyerga,\ngermanium, carbamide and, as needed, micro-dose chemotherapy, amino acids IV, vitamin C to bowel tolerance, selenium at 500-\n1 ,000 mcg per day, and vitamin A at 300,000 IU\nas beta carotene",
