        facet: "protocol",
        question: "How do you treat leukorrhea (abnormal vaginal discharge)?",
        answer_short: "Wallach treats leukorrhea by targeting the causative organism with vaginal triple-sulfonamide creams, oxytetracycline suppositories, vinegar douches and 1.5% hydrogen peroxide (Oxy-Toddy), adding systemic antibiotics when gonorrhea is the cause.",
        answer: "Leukorrhea (vaginal discharge) is a nonspecific vaginal discharge containing mucus and white blood cells, occasionally blood-tinged; it can be caused by Candida albicans, Hemophilus vaginalis, Streptococcus, Staphylococcus (the organism behind toxic-shock syndrome with incorrectly used tampons), or Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Specific diagnosis requires culture and/or microscopy of the discharge. Treatment targets the causative organism: vaginal triple-sulfonamide creams, oxytetracycline vaginal suppositories, vinegar douches (30 milliliters per pint of water), and 1.5% hydrogen peroxide (as Oxy-Toddy); gonorrhea also requires systemic antibiotics. Wallach notes that any venereal disease must be reported to the Public Health Department.",
        verbatim: "Treatment of leukorrhea should include specific treatment for the causative organism (i.e.,\nvaginal application of triple sulfonamide\ncreams, oxytetracycline vaginal suppositories, vaginal douches with vinegar 30 ml/pint\nof water, 1.5 % H202 as Oxy Toddy). Gonorrhea will require systemic antibiotics as well\n(please note that any venereal disease must be\nreported to the Public Health Department",
