        answer_short: "Wallach stresses prevention \u2014 make a puncture wound bleed, clean with soap and water, disinfect with 3% hydrogen peroxide, and take cramp bark tea \u2014 and says once lockjaw develops it needs tetanus antitoxin, wound cleaning, IV penicillin or tetracycline, sedation, and IV fluids.",
        answer: "For lockjaw (tetanus) \u2014 caused by the toxin of Clostridium tetani, a normal inhabitant of animal manure that enters through puncture wounds and paralyzes the voluntary muscles including the jaw (masseter) muscles \u2014 Wallach stresses prevention as the best option: make the puncture wound bleed by squeezing it, open small foot or hand punctures to clean with soap and water, then disinfect with 3% hydrogen peroxide, and take cramp bark tea (Viburnum opulus) in tablespoon doses as needed; he says these minimal steps prevent lockjaw almost 100% of the time. Once it develops, treatment requires tetanus-neutralizing (non-fixed) antitoxin, cleaning and disinfecting the infected wound with hydrogen peroxide, penicillin at two million units intravenously every 6 hours OR tetracycline at 500 milligrams intravenously every 6 hours, sedation to control muscle spasms, and intravenous fluids and electrolytes until the patient can eat and drink on their own.",
        verbatim: "Treatment of lockjaw after it develops requires the use of tetanus-neutralizing (non-fixed) antitoxin, clean infected wound, disinfect\nwound with hydrogen peroxide, penicillin at\ntwo million u IV q 6 h or tetracycline at 500 mg\nrv q6 h, sedation to control muscle spasms, IV\nfluids and electrolytes until the patient can eat\nand drink on their own",
