        answer: "For lupus (systemic lupus erythematosus, SLE) \u2014 a connective-tissue disease with 90% of cases in women in their 30s \u2014 Wallach argues the autoimmune antibody attack is a result of the disease and its abnormal proteins, not its cause. He notes the classic sudden onset of fever, fatigue, arthritis and joint pain (often misdiagnosed as rheumatoid arthritis), a facial 'butterfly' rash, severe hair loss and papular skin lesions, and recommends the pulse test to find food allergies (wheat, cow's milk and soy are most common). His treatment is: avoid offending food allergens and rotate non-allergic foods; autoimmune urine therapy; chelation with total nutrition for 15-25 infusions; essential fatty acids (salmon oil and flaxseed oil) at 5 grams three times a day; selenium at 500-1,000 micrograms per day; carnitine (printed 'B-carnitine') at 500 milligrams per day; avoid beef, dairy and alfalfa; and daily doses of plant-derived colloidal minerals.",
