        answer: "Measles (rubeola) is a highly contagious viral disease of sudden onset with cough, nasal drainage, conjunctivitis, Koplik's spots (eruptions on the mouth and lip lining), and a pimple-like rash starting on the head and neck and spreading to the body, arms and legs; fever to 104\xB0F is common, and pneumonia and encephalitis are unusual but potentially fatal complications. Wallach notes it now affects mainly teenagers and young adults, incubates 7-14 days (the diagnostic Koplik's spots appear about four days after the fever), and that having had measles confers lifelong immunity. His treatment is topical over-the-counter anti-itch products (for example Caladryl) plus herbs: salves of marigold (Calendula officinalis), and taken orally columbine (Aquilegia vulgaris), yarrow (Achillea millefolium) and pleurisy root (Asclepias tuberosa).",
