        question: "How does Wallach treat memory loss (senile dementia, Korsakoff's, Alzheimer's)?",
        answer_short: "First rule out food allergies (pulse test) and hypoglycemia (six-hour glucose tolerance test); then avoid the offending foods, sugar and alcohol, and add chromium, selenium, vitamin E, B-complex, plus the nootropics vasopressin nasal spray, centrophenoxine, piracetam (with choline) and hydergine.",
        answer: "For memory loss (senile dementia, Korsakoff's syndrome, Alzheimer's disease) Wallach says there are many causes that should each be looked up separately, including food allergies and hypoglycemia; he advises the pulse test for food allergies/sensitivities and a six-hour glucose tolerance test to rule hypoglycemia in or out. His treatment is: avoid offending food allergens, sugar and alcohol; chromium at 25-200 micrograms three times a day; selenium at 200-1,000 micrograms per day; vitamin E at 800-1,200 international units per day; B-complex at 50 milligrams three times a day; Diapid (vasopressin) nasal spray at 12-16 units per day; Lucidril (centrophenoxine) at 4.4-8.0 grams per day; Dinagen (piracetam) at 1.6-4.8 grams per day (take choline with it); and hydergine at 9-20 milligrams per day.",
