        question: "How do you treat painful periods?",
        answer_short: "For painful or heavy periods (which Wallach usually blames on essential-fatty-acid deficiency) he recommends salmon and flaxseed oil, vitamin E, selenium, and iron, plus over-the-counter products or herbs.",
        answer: "Wallach describes menarche/menstruation: the regular monthly cycle alternating ovulation (mid-cycle, about day 14) with the 3-7 day 'period' (discharge of blood and uterine lining). He notes periods often stop in athletes and women who drop below 20% body fat \u2014 of no health consequence in itself, but a signal of being too thin for normal cycling and ovulation. Painful periods (which he usually attributes to essential-fatty-acid deficiency) can be severe enough to force bed rest. His treatment for painful or excessive ('heavy flow') menses: over-the-counter products (for example Midol) and/or herbs \u2014 snapdragon (Linaria vulgaris), bethroot (Trillium erectum), black cohosh (Cimicifuga racemosa), blue cohosh (Caulophyllum thalictroides), tamarac (Larix americana), ragwort (Senecio fuchsii), lady's mantle (Alchemilla vulgaris), St. John's wort (Hypericum perforatum), shepherd's purse (Capsella bursa-pastoris), white deadnettle (Lamium album) and yarrow (Achillea millefolium); plus essential fatty acids (salmon oil and flaxseed oil) at 5 grams three times a day, vitamin E at 800-1,200 international units per day, selenium at 500-1,000 micrograms per day, and iron at 25-50 milligrams per day (especially for vegetarian women who don't eat red meat or liver regularly).",
