        question: "Is meningitis a medical emergency?",
        answer_short: "Wallach warns that meningitis is not a self-help disease \u2014 if you suspect it, get the patient to an emergency room immediately and demand help, because patients often die after waiting hours to be seen.",
        answer: "Meningitis (infection of the covering of the brain and spinal cord) can be caused by many organisms \u2014 bacteria (Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus/Diplococcus pneumoniae, Listeria monocytogenes), fungi, and hundreds of viruses. Wallach stresses it is especially dangerous because infants often show only fever and lethargy, and the blood-brain barrier keeps most medications out of the meninges and brain. Symptoms vary but usually include sore throat, fever, headache, stiff neck and vomiting, and children and adults can become critically ill within 6-24 hours of the first symptoms. He warns emphatically that this is NOT a self-help disease: if you suspect meningitis, get the patient to an emergency room immediately and demand help, because patients often die after waiting hours in a waiting room. Treatment requires rapid diagnosis (spinal tap) and injection of antibiotics into the subdural space (under the meninges), total intravenous fluid and electrolyte support, and frequently mechanical respirators.",
        verbatim: "If you suspect meningitis, this is\none disease that requires rapid diagnosis and\ntreatment GET PATIENT TO AN EMERGENCY\nROOMSTAT!!! DEMAND IMMEDIATE HELP!!!\nMeningitis patients often die because they\nhave waited 2-4 hours in an emergency waiting room",
