        answer_short: "Wallach's treatment is to manually remove the obstruction with a finger, perform a Heimlich maneuver, and as a last resort perform an emergency tracheotomy, followed by CPR if the heart has also stopped.",
        answer: "Obstruction of the airways (choking) is heralded by the 'universal choking sign' \u2014 both hands clutched at the throat and loss of speech; it happens often in restaurants (a 'restaurant coronary' mistaken for a heart attack). Prevention: don't talk while chewing and swallowing, especially meat and firm raw vegetables. Treatment: (1) put a finger into the back of the throat and manually remove the obstruction; (2) perform a Heimlich maneuver (stand behind the patient, lock your arms around them with hands just below the xiphoid cartilage and thrust sharply; for babies use hands only). If the obstruction cannot be cleared, Wallach describes an emergency tracheotomy as a last-resort lifesaving measure \u2014 a dead-center incision midway between the base of the larynx and the notch where the collarbones meet the breastbone, large enough for the empty barrel of a ballpoint pen to maintain airflow \u2014 followed by CPR (cardiopulmonary resuscitation) if the heart has also stopped.",
