        facet: "protocol",
        question: "How do you treat Paget's disease of bone?",
        answer_short: "Wallach's treatment aims to correct the dietary calcium-to-phosphorus ratio \u2014 cutting red meat and phosphate soft drinks, lowering vitamin D and sun exposure, raising calcium to 2,000 mg and magnesium to 1,000 mg a day, adding betaine HCl and pancreatic enzymes before meals, and following the baseline supplement program.",
        answer: "Osteitis fibrosa (nutritional secondary hyperparathyroidism), also known as Paget's disease of bone, is a bone disease \u2014 more common in men \u2014 in which hard bone is lost and replaced by fibrous connective tissue. It often shows up first in the jaw (separating and loosening the teeth), then in the weight-bearing bones of the pelvis, femur, and tibia, with loss of height and a 'dowager's hump' (kyphosis). Mainstream medicine calls the cause unknown, but in animals it comes from a reversed dietary calcium-to-phosphorus ratio together with relatively large amounts of vitamin D. It is diagnosed by x-ray and by elevated blood alkaline phosphatase and parathyroid hormone. Treatment should include a vigorous effort to correct the dietary calcium/phosphorus ratio: reduce or eliminate red meat, avoid phosphate-containing soft drinks and supplements, reduce vitamin D intake and sun exposure (hats, long sleeves), raise calcium and magnesium intake to 2,000 milligrams and 1,000 milligrams per day, take betaine hydrochloride and pancreatic enzymes at 75-200 milligrams three times a day fifteen minutes before meals, and follow the baseline nutritional supplement program.",
        verbatim: "vigorous effort to conect the dietary calcium/\nphosphorus ratio, reduce or eliminate red\nmeat intake, avoid phosphate containing soft\ndrinks, avoid all phosphate containing supplements, reduce vitamin D intake and exposure\n(i.e., hats, long sleeve shirts, etc.), increase\ncalcium and magnesium intake to 2,000 mg\nand 1,000 mg per day",
        page: 157,
        book_id: "lets-play-doctor",
