        question: "How much leucine does Wallach recommend for Parkinson's disease?",
        answer_short: "For drug-induced Parkinsonism, Wallach's protocol includes leucine at 10 grams a day, alongside octacosanol, L-methionine, L-tyrosine, DL-phenylalanine, B-vitamins, essential fatty acids, and digestive enzymes.",
        answer: "Parkinsonism (shaking palsy) is caused by degeneration of the basal ganglia of the brain; its symptoms are relentlessly progressive and include muscular rigidity, lack of purposeful movement, and a 'pill rolling' tremor of the thumb and index finger. The cause of true Parkinson's disease is unknown, but many things can cause Parkinsonism-like symptoms: certain drugs (phenothiazines, haloperidol, reserpine), carbon-monoxide poisoning, and excessive manganese supplementation. For drug-induced Parkinsonism, treatment should include eliminating the offending drug, then octacosanol at 300 micrograms three times a day, Neuro-Gen, leucine 10 grams a day, L-methionine 5 grams a day, essential fatty acids one tablespoon twice a day, L-tyrosine 100 milligrams a day, DL-phenylalanine 100 milligrams three times a day, vitamin B-1 at 200 milligrams three times a day, vitamin B-6 at 100 milligrams three times a day, betaine hydrochloride and pancreatic enzymes at 75-200 milligrams three times a day before meals, and the baseline nutritional supplement program. He warns that the standard L-Dopa and carbidopa aggravate and speed up Parkinson's in a significant number of cases and have no benefit in more than half.",
