        question: "What does iron deficiency look like on a blood test?",
        answer_short: "Iron deficiency shows up as decreased hemoglobin and small red cells \u2014 the picture of a microcytic anemia.",
        answer: "On a complete blood count, Wallach says iron deficiency shows up as decreased hemoglobin (HGB), the protein in red blood cells that binds oxygen and carbon dioxide. It also produces very small red blood cells and a reduced mean corpuscular volume (MCV) \u2014 the classic picture of a microcytic anemia. By contrast, larger cells or elevated values point instead to B-12 or folic acid deficiency.",
        verbatim: "HGB (hemoglobin) is the specialized protein\nin the RBC that actually binds with oxygen and\ncarbon dioxide. Increased values are seen in\nB-12 deficiency (because there are fewer cells)\nand decreased values are characteristic of iron\ndeficiency.",
