        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "What causes osteoporosis?",
        answer_short: "A calcium deficiency disease. Too little dietary calcium \u2014 worsened by shortfalls of magnesium, boron, copper and sulfur and by excess phosphorus \u2014 forces the body to strip calcium from the bones until they thin and break.",
        answer: "Wallach classifies osteoporosis as a calcium deficiency disease \u2014 he calls it \u201Cthe well known\u201D endpoint of a whole family of calcium-deficiency conditions. The root problem is chronically inadequate dietary calcium (he says the entire scope of American diets is critically deficient in calcium), made worse by co-factor deficiencies of magnesium, boron, copper and sulfur and by the excess phosphorus of the modern diet, which raises calcium requirements and aggravates osteoporosis. When dietary calcium falls short, the body draws calcium out of the bones to keep blood levels up \u2014 thinning the skeleton until it fractures.",
        verbatim: "The more common calcium deficiency\ndiseases (Table 11-10) are easy to recog-\nnize and run from poor clotting time of the\nblood when you nick yourself shaving (cal-\ncium is a co-factor in the clotting mecha-\nnism), arthritis (which the allopaths treat\nwith pain killers), to the well known\nosteoporosis",
