        answer_short: "Chiefly a calcium deficiency \u2014 Wallach points to 30-plus studies tying low calcium to high blood pressure \u2014 with magnesium and boron shortfalls (and excess dietary phosphorus) making it worse.",
        answer: "Wallach attributes high blood pressure primarily to chronic calcium deficiency. He cites McCarron's 1980 finding \u2014 backed, he says, by more than 30 later studies \u2014 that calcium deficiency is a cause of hypertension, and notes that serum ionized calcium runs consistently lower in people with untreated high blood pressure. He also lists hypertension among the calcium deficiency diseases, with magnesium and boron as complicating-factor deficiencies, and warns that a high-phosphorus diet aggravates high blood pressure.",
        verbatim: "In 1980, McCarron, et al, theorized that\nchronic calcium deficiency probably led to\nhypertension. More than 30 subsequent\nstudies supported the original theory of\ncalcium deficiency as the cause of\nhypertension, additionally recent studies\nhave shown that serum ionized calcium is\nconsistently lower in humans with un-\ntreated hypertension",
