        facet: "physiology",
        question: "What deficiencies cause diabetes?",
        answer_short: "Wallach calls adult-onset diabetes a chromium-and-vanadium deficiency (his table adds zinc), usually driven by poor absorption and sugar-flushed minerals, not by bad genes.",
        answer: 'Wallach holds that adult-onset (type 2) diabetes and hypoglycemia are "in reality" deficiencies of chromium and vanadium, frequently created by malabsorption from celiac-type changes in the small intestine (his degenerative-disease table also lists zinc). Chromium is tightly bound up with GTF, the glucose tolerance factor that lets insulin do its work; vanadium "appears to function like insulin," altering cell-membrane ion transport so insulin receptors become more sensitive and glucose is properly oxidized and stored. Because refined sugar and flour dump these minerals out through the urine, he argues the disease is largely dietary, and that restoring chromium and vanadium can reduce or even eliminate most cases of adult-onset diabetes.',
        verbatim: "Table 7-8. Common Degenerative Diseases of Humans and Their Cause.\nOsteoporosis: Ca, Mg, B, Cu, S\nArthritis (Ankylosing spondylitis): Ca, Mg, B, Cu, S\nHypertension: Ca, Mg, B\nBone spurs, heel spurs, Ca deposits: Ca, Mg, B\nCancer: Se\nDiabetes: Cr, Va, Zn\nAneurysms: Cu\nCardiomyopathy: Se\nCataracts: Se\nDeafness: Sn",
