        facet: "stance",
        question: "What causes aneurysms according to Wallach?",
        answer_short: "Aneurysms trace to copper deficiency: without copper-dependent lysyl oxidase, artery walls can't build strong elastin, so they weaken and blow out.",
        answer: `Wallach attributes aneurysms to copper deficiency. Copper is the cofactor for lysyl oxidase, the enzyme that cross-links pro-elastin into elastin, the protein that gives artery walls their tensile (elastic) strength. When copper is deficient, lysyl oxidase activity falls, elastin isn't properly formed, and the weakened arterial walls balloon and rupture as aneurysms. He points to famous "ruptured aneurysm" deaths (Albert Einstein and Conway Twitty among them) as copper-deficiency cases, and notes that four to six of every 100 Americans autopsied have died of a ruptured aneurysm, with another 40 percent carrying aneurysms that had not yet ruptured.`,
        verbatim: "Table 7-8. Common Degenerative Diseases of Humans and Their Cause.\nOsteoporosis: Ca, Mg, B, Cu, S\nArthritis (Ankylosing spondylitis): Ca, Mg, B, Cu, S\nHypertension: Ca, Mg, B\nBone spurs, heel spurs, Ca deposits: Ca, Mg, B\nCancer: Se\nDiabetes: Cr, Va, Zn\nAneurysms: Cu\nCardiomyopathy: Se\nCataracts: Se\nDeafness: Sn",
