        answer_short: "Yes \u2014 Wallach treats tin as an essential mineral whose deficiency produces hearing loss (shown in animal studies), and his disease-cause table lists deafness under tin.",
        answer: `Yes \u2014 in Wallach's framework tin is an essential trace mineral, and hearing loss is one of its deficiency signs. He cites Schwarz's work (which established tin as essential in 1970): rats on a tin-deficient diet showed "poor growth, reduced feeding efficiency, hearing loss, and bilateral (male pattern) hair loss," and were physiologically normal when tin was restored. His table of degenerative diseases accordingly lists deafness under tin (Sn), and he ties the documented generational decline in men's hearing to this deficiency.`,
