        question: "Is chicken soup actually useful when you're sick with the flu?",
        answer_short: "Yes. Wallach holds up chicken soup \u2014 with the herbs echinacea and goldenseal plus a dose of bismuth \u2014 as the safe, effective nutritional way to handle the flu (which he frames as dehydration and diarrhea). His cautionary contrast is George Washington, who had influenza but was killed by his doctors, not the illness: in a little over 12 hours they drained him of 80 to 90 ounces of blood (6 pints), dosed him with calomel (mercury) and emetic tartar (antimony), and blistered his skin and throat, until Washington himself begged to be allowed to die in peace. The lesson Wallach draws is blunt \u2014 a grandmother's bowl of chicken soup would have served the 'Father of our Country' better than the best physicians of his day.",
        answer: "Wallach holds up chicken soup \u2014 with the herbs echinacea and goldenseal plus a dose of bismuth \u2014 as the safe, effective nutritional way to handle the flu (which he frames as dehydration and diarrhea). His cautionary contrast is George Washington, who had influenza but was killed by his doctors, not the illness: in a little over 12 hours they drained him of 80 to 90 ounces of blood (6 pints), dosed him with calomel (mercury) and emetic tartar (antimony), and blistered his skin and throat, until Washington himself begged to be allowed to die in peace. The lesson Wallach draws is blunt \u2014 a grandmother's bowl of chicken soup would have served the 'Father of our Country' better than the best physicians of his day.",
