        answer_short: "Wallach describes sodium chloride (the cell salt Natrum Mur) as regulating cellular moisture by attracting and distributing water.",
        answer: "Wallach presents sodium chloride \u2014 known in cell-salt terms as Natrum Mur, the chloride of soda \u2014 as a constituent of every liquid and solid part of the body. He says it regulates the moisture inside cells by attracting and distributing water, and since the body is about 70% water, that water would be inert and useless without it. A deficiency, in this account, throws off the body's water balance, leaving a watery, bloated appearance.",
        verbatim: "Natrum Mur (Chloride of soda or Sodium chloride) - this tissue salt is a constituant of every liquid and solid part of the body. It\ntegulates the degree of moisture within the cells by virtue of its property of attracting and distributing water. The human body is\ncomposed of about 70 % of water, which in the absence of Natrum Nur, would be inert and useless. Any deficiency of this tissue salt\nCauses an imbalance of water in the human organism. The patient then shows a watery, bloated appearance; is languid, drowsy and\ninclined to watery exudations; and sometimes there is the opposite condition of excessive dryness.",
