        answer_short: "Wallach opens his minerals chapter with a quote noting salt joins an explosive metal (sodium) and a lethal gas (chlorine), yet becomes an absolute necessity for life.",
        answer: "To frame chlorine's paradox, Wallach opens his minerals chapter with a quote from G. Young in National Geographic: salt is a blend of sodium\u2014a metal so unstable it bursts into flame in water\u2014and chlorine, a lethal gas. When swallowed, the quote notes, the blend forms hydrochloric acid in the stomach; rather than being suicidal, it is an absolute necessity for life. Wallach uses the passage to underscore that a dangerous element becomes indispensable in its dietary form.",
