        question: "What does tyrosine have to do with hair and skin color?",
        answer_short: "Tyrosine is the raw material for melanin, the pigment in hair and skin; the enzyme tyrosinase acts on it with copper as a cofactor, so a copper shortage can turn hair white, silver, or gray.",
        answer: "Tyrosine is the starting material the body uses to make melanin, the pigment that colors hair and skin. The enzyme tyrosinase acts on tyrosine to build the pigment, and this reaction requires copper as a cofactor. Wallach points out the practical consequence: when copper is deficient, the reaction stalls and hair turns white, silver, or gray. He also notes that red hair is a different case -- its pigment (tricosiverin) is an iron-based compound rather than ordinary melanin.",
        verbatim: "Hair pigment\nis a type of melanin which is formed in the\npresence of the enzyme tyrosinase and its\neffects on the essential amino acid tyrosine\n(copper is required as a cofactor for this\nreaction - therefore a copper deficiency re-\nsults in white, silver or gray hair).",
