        question: "How does the body use hydrogen peroxide to fight infection?",
        answer_short: "Wallach says neutrophils (white blood cells) make hydrogen peroxide as a 'lethal weapon' to destroy germs and cancer cells, while the enzyme catalase protects healthy tissue from the excess.",
        answer: "Wallach describes hydrogen peroxide as one of the body's own built-in weapons. Neutrophils, the white blood cells that hunt down viruses, bacteria, yeast, fungi, parasites and cancer cells, are packed with tiny organelles called peroxisomes whose only job is to make hydrogen peroxide and eject it onto the captured target to destroy it. Because neutrophils are 'sloppy' and spill their excess peroxide into the bloodstream, Wallach notes the body is protected by an enzyme, catalase, that coats the red blood cells and blood-vessel walls and rapidly breaks stray hydrogen peroxide down into water and singlet oxygen.",
        verbatim: "Neutrophils, a type of white blood cell that help defend us by identifying, engulfing and destroying invading micro-organisms (i.e.- virus, bacteria, yeast, fungus, etc.), parasites and cancer cells, use hydrogen peroxide as their \u201Clethal weapon.\u201D Neutrophils are packed with small organelles ( microscopic organs) called peroxisomes, whose sole function is to produce hydrogen peroxide and eject it onto the captured pathogen or cancer cell for the specific purpose of destroying it. Neutrophils tend to be very sloppy, dribbling their over-production of hydrogen peroxide freely into the blood stream.",
