        answer: "Silver's germ-killing power gave rise to one of medicine's most enduring newborn safeguards. Wallach relays from the older medical literature that in 1884 Carl Sigmund Crede, a German professor of obstetrics, instituted the practice of placing a single prophylactic drop of 2% silver nitrate in each eye of every infant at the moment of birth, to prevent infantile (gonorrheal) conjunctivitis -- the eye infection then called ophthalmia neonatorum. It grew out of a longer tradition of using silver nitrate, or 'lunar caustic,' to treat wounds, ulcers, and inflamed eyes.",
